[{:db/id 17592186124594 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c9126714-6e57-5593-b5b4-4801efbfb113"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c9126714-6e57-5593-b5b4-4801efbfb113" :question/id #uuid "c9126714-6e57-5593-b5b4-4801efbfb113" :question/math-academy-id "q-95111" :question/problem "Given that $q(x,y)=xy,$ find $\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta },$ where $(x,y)$ are Cartesian coordinates and $(r,\\theta )$ are the corresponding plane polar coordinates." :question/worked-solution "Applying the multivariable chain rule for the function $q(x,y)$ with two independent variables $r$ and $\\theta ,$ where $x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ and $y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =\\frac{∂q}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂\\theta }+\\frac{∂q}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂\\theta } \\\\  & =-\\frac{∂q}{∂x}r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{∂q}{∂y}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $q$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x}=y,\\,\\frac{∂q}{∂y}=x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $r$ and $\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂x} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta , \\\\ \\frac{∂q}{∂y} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting these into our chain rule formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂q}{∂\\theta } & =-(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =-{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ) \\\\  & ={r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-53289756-546a-50f2-80e0-c0a1e0a48184"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-53289756-546a-50f2-80e0-c0a1e0a48184" :answer-field/id #uuid "53289756-546a-50f2-80e0-c0a1e0a48184" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5c05a8fd-c462-59f0-82be-578cd7e2ef16" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c3a54554-40d8-5b1a-a681-1f39ccace6c7" "answer-5c05a8fd-c462-59f0-82be-578cd7e2ef16" "answer-12433c4a-668f-57da-9582-b6ee6515dc42" "answer-d353fe5f-d6bc-5d38-8a44-d344cb894fbe" "answer-f972d2aa-60ae-5ae2-a173-f83d6fe3630e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c3a54554-40d8-5b1a-a681-1f39ccace6c7" :answer/id #uuid "c3a54554-40d8-5b1a-a681-1f39ccace6c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5c05a8fd-c462-59f0-82be-578cd7e2ef16" :answer/id #uuid "5c05a8fd-c462-59f0-82be-578cd7e2ef16" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12433c4a-668f-57da-9582-b6ee6515dc42" :answer/id #uuid "12433c4a-668f-57da-9582-b6ee6515dc42" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d353fe5f-d6bc-5d38-8a44-d344cb894fbe" :answer/id #uuid "d353fe5f-d6bc-5d38-8a44-d344cb894fbe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f972d2aa-60ae-5ae2-a173-f83d6fe3630e" :answer/id #uuid "f972d2aa-60ae-5ae2-a173-f83d6fe3630e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-8192fdb4-5ea5-58a8-b9e0-07e9449bea2f" :activity/id #uuid "8192fdb4-5ea5-58a8-b9e0-07e9449bea2f" :activity/title "The Multivariable Chain Rule With Polar Coordinates" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048635 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-c0e7d08d-e45f-545c-a6af-cb153d69442e" "step-9478d728-7a8c-5a18-beaa-daba72c1c2d7" "step-b30ffaf9-b23b-569c-9c7e-cc9390ec51b6" "step-bad45fb5-6e01-535a-822a-7b2bcb1da7a2" "step-fc30f928-6c4b-5fd5-b548-a7acd91c520f"] :activity/first-step "step-c0e7d08d-e45f-545c-a6af-cb153d69442e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-c0e7d08d-e45f-545c-a6af-cb153d69442e" :step/id #uuid "c0e7d08d-e45f-545c-a6af-cb153d69442e" :step/content 17592186360535 :step/next "step-9478d728-7a8c-5a18-beaa-daba72c1c2d7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-9478d728-7a8c-5a18-beaa-daba72c1c2d7" :step/id #uuid "9478d728-7a8c-5a18-beaa-daba72c1c2d7" :step/content 17592186125248 :step/next "step-b30ffaf9-b23b-569c-9c7e-cc9390ec51b6" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b30ffaf9-b23b-569c-9c7e-cc9390ec51b6" :step/id #uuid "b30ffaf9-b23b-569c-9c7e-cc9390ec51b6" :step/content "question-c9126714-6e57-5593-b5b4-4801efbfb113" :step/next "step-bad45fb5-6e01-535a-822a-7b2bcb1da7a2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-bad45fb5-6e01-535a-822a-7b2bcb1da7a2" :step/id #uuid "bad45fb5-6e01-535a-822a-7b2bcb1da7a2" :step/content 17592186360565 :step/next "step-fc30f928-6c4b-5fd5-b548-a7acd91c520f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fc30f928-6c4b-5fd5-b548-a7acd91c520f" :step/id #uuid "fc30f928-6c4b-5fd5-b548-a7acd91c520f" :step/content 17592186360567 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096658 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d4c0de90-e894-5c05-811c-ac221194e0c5" "question-1571510a-8d3e-5007-9b28-e17c3cb5bde3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-d4c0de90-e894-5c05-811c-ac221194e0c5" :question/id #uuid "d4c0de90-e894-5c05-811c-ac221194e0c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-112129" :question/problem "A five gallons water bottle is leaking its content. The number of gallons of water left inside the bottle after $t$ minutes is given by the formula $W(t)=5\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{t}.$ How many gallons of water will be inside the bottle after $3$ minutes?" :question/worked-solution "To get the number of gallons of water inside the bottle after $3$ minutes, we evaluate the function at $t=3.$ Substituting $t=3$ into the function $W(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W(3) & =5\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{5})}^{3} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (\\frac{{1}^{3}}{{5}^{3}}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{125} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{125} \\\\  & =0.04.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, after $3$ minutes, there will be $0.04$ gallons of water inside the bottle." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6bbf46a9-a271-5e94-8244-41b134f4a887"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6bbf46a9-a271-5e94-8244-41b134f4a887" :answer-field/id #uuid "6bbf46a9-a271-5e94-8244-41b134f4a887" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0f7d7512-00dd-53a1-a339-e816ce6f9bc3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a758b6c6-f6c3-5428-a865-833457877121" "answer-0f7d7512-00dd-53a1-a339-e816ce6f9bc3" "answer-4a011cf2-f11f-556b-9630-c5475d45f2d3" "answer-d808d943-003d-59e8-a1b2-bb5ce6ae207f" "answer-9528fa7d-db24-588f-95a3-5f14223601ca"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a758b6c6-f6c3-5428-a865-833457877121" :answer/id #uuid "a758b6c6-f6c3-5428-a865-833457877121" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0.02$ gallons" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f7d7512-00dd-53a1-a339-e816ce6f9bc3" :answer/id #uuid "0f7d7512-00dd-53a1-a339-e816ce6f9bc3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0.04$ gallons" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a011cf2-f11f-556b-9630-c5475d45f2d3" :answer/id #uuid "4a011cf2-f11f-556b-9630-c5475d45f2d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0.2$ gallons" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d808d943-003d-59e8-a1b2-bb5ce6ae207f" :answer/id #uuid "d808d943-003d-59e8-a1b2-bb5ce6ae207f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$0.4$ gallons" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9528fa7d-db24-588f-95a3-5f14223601ca" :answer/id #uuid "9528fa7d-db24-588f-95a3-5f14223601ca" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$1$ gallon" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1571510a-8d3e-5007-9b28-e17c3cb5bde3" :question/id #uuid "1571510a-8d3e-5007-9b28-e17c3cb5bde3" :question/math-academy-id "q-112148" :question/problem "The number of subscribers to a video channel is given by $S(t)=5\\cdot (2.4)^{t},$ where $t$ is the time in days since the channel was opened. How many subscribers will the channel have after a week? Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "To get the number of subscribers to the video channel after a week ($7$ days), we evaluate the function at $t=7.$ Substituting $t=7$ into the function $S(t)$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S(7) & =5\\cdot (2.4)^{7} \\\\  & ≈2\\,293.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, after a week, the video channel will have $2\\,293$ subscribers." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-595c2d53-60ff-5406-b569-8b0372ade376"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-595c2d53-60ff-5406-b569-8b0372ade376" :answer-field/id #uuid "595c2d53-60ff-5406-b569-8b0372ade376" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-363f8683-7ce6-519e-b516-88ec0639bc77" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7999765f-0877-5da0-8674-68c82949589f" "answer-d6f48a1a-dec7-5522-a1f4-4f63c0ce08a6" "answer-363f8683-7ce6-519e-b516-88ec0639bc77" "answer-bc607cfb-7458-58ee-9fe1-447d861318c6" "answer-83349184-0745-5f0a-adb8-dc217a25b73d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7999765f-0877-5da0-8674-68c82949589f" :answer/id #uuid "7999765f-0877-5da0-8674-68c82949589f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$11\\,185$ subscribers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6f48a1a-dec7-5522-a1f4-4f63c0ce08a6" :answer/id #uuid "d6f48a1a-dec7-5522-a1f4-4f63c0ce08a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$234\\,375$ subscribers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-363f8683-7ce6-519e-b516-88ec0639bc77" :answer/id #uuid "363f8683-7ce6-519e-b516-88ec0639bc77" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$2\\,293$ subscribers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc607cfb-7458-58ee-9fe1-447d861318c6" :answer/id #uuid "bc607cfb-7458-58ee-9fe1-447d861318c6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$32\\,805$ subscribers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-83349184-0745-5f0a-adb8-dc217a25b73d" :answer/id #uuid "83349184-0745-5f0a-adb8-dc217a25b73d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$3\\,645$ subscribers" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096659 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4fda74bd-4783-5b76-9154-8ddd3936c772"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-4fda74bd-4783-5b76-9154-8ddd3936c772" :question/id #uuid "4fda74bd-4783-5b76-9154-8ddd3936c772" :question/math-academy-id "q-218594" :question/problem "If $f(x)=A\\cdot {2}^{x}$ and $f(6)=192,$ then $A=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We substitute $x=6$ and $f(x)=192$ into the expression for $f(x)$ and then solve for $A,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}192 & =A\\cdot {2}^{6} \\\\ \\frac{192}{{2}^{6}} & =A \\\\ \\frac{192}{64} & =A \\\\ 3 & =A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6b57ab6-1b1d-515d-affc-7350d9f0ce89"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6b57ab6-1b1d-515d-affc-7350d9f0ce89" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6b57ab6-1b1d-515d-affc-7350d9f0ce89" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9063cde2-3330-509e-ae49-cc65532f4b9a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9063cde2-3330-509e-ae49-cc65532f4b9a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9063cde2-3330-509e-ae49-cc65532f4b9a" :answer/id #uuid "9063cde2-3330-509e-ae49-cc65532f4b9a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-7c388d04-2b80-59a8-b4dd-4cabcf69a693" :activity/id #uuid "7c388d04-2b80-59a8-b4dd-4cabcf69a693" :activity/title "Exponential Functions" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049619 :activity/course 17592186046648 :activity/steps ["step-f31a4f50-f5a8-571a-bc6d-1143b83e36a5" "step-2c28505c-84c4-5461-afbd-0a3de162478c" "step-d1d64943-9a98-5436-a9cf-ea3fcf7adb24"] :activity/first-step "step-f31a4f50-f5a8-571a-bc6d-1143b83e36a5" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-f31a4f50-f5a8-571a-bc6d-1143b83e36a5" :step/id #uuid "f31a4f50-f5a8-571a-bc6d-1143b83e36a5" :step/content "question-d4c0de90-e894-5c05-811c-ac221194e0c5" :step/next "step-2c28505c-84c4-5461-afbd-0a3de162478c" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-2c28505c-84c4-5461-afbd-0a3de162478c" :step/id #uuid "2c28505c-84c4-5461-afbd-0a3de162478c" :step/content "question-1571510a-8d3e-5007-9b28-e17c3cb5bde3" :step/next "step-d1d64943-9a98-5436-a9cf-ea3fcf7adb24" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-d1d64943-9a98-5436-a9cf-ea3fcf7adb24" :step/id #uuid "d1d64943-9a98-5436-a9cf-ea3fcf7adb24" :step/content "question-4fda74bd-4783-5b76-9154-8ddd3936c772" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186125238 :question/answer-fields 17592186124028] {:db/id 17592186357256 :answer-field/correct 17592186355077 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125828 :tutorial/content "Consider the quadric surface\n\n$$\nz^2 = x^2 + y^2\n$$\n\nwhose graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/d5/d5a23dfa8bf6a0efec59763b9a2ec6d22d878f75babdbed9fc04dc8a7106f6ba.png)\n\nThis is an example of an **elliptic cone**. It has its **vertex** at the origin, and the $z$-axis is the **axis of symmetry** of the cone.\n\nMore generally, the equation of an elliptic cone whose vertex is at $(x_0, y_0, z_0)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(z-z_0)^2 = \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124697 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-91c6ce97-3ba3-5af9-8b62-e9a8d179a50b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-91c6ce97-3ba3-5af9-8b62-e9a8d179a50b" :question/id #uuid "91c6ce97-3ba3-5af9-8b62-e9a8d179a50b" :question/math-academy-id "q-131070" :question/problem "Suppose that $f(x)$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}, & x<1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{x+1}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn which of the following intervals can the extreme value theorem be applied to the function?\n\nI. $[-5,1)$\n\nII. $[0,5]$\n\nIII. $[5,10]$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that the extreme value theorem is only applicable to closed intervals on which the function is continuous.\n\nSo, let's check whether the function is continuous on each of the intervals.\n\n- The interval $[-5,1)$ is not closed, so the extreme value theorem is not applicable.\n\n- Now, consider $[0,5].$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{{x}^{2}-x}{{x}^{2}-1}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{x(x-1)}{(x-1)(x+1)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{x}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to 1}(\\frac{1}{x+1})=\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the limits from the left and from the right of $x=1$ are the same. Hence, $f(x)$ is continuous on the interval $[0,5],$ and the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\n- Finally, consider $[5,10].$ In this interval $f(x)=\\frac{1}{x+1}$ and so $f(x)$ is continuous on $[5,10].$ Therefore, the extreme value theorem is applicable on this interval.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0afcfbf6-047c-53c2-8a23-be6af1196c25"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0afcfbf6-047c-53c2-8a23-be6af1196c25" :answer-field/id #uuid "0afcfbf6-047c-53c2-8a23-be6af1196c25" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8795d4c5-ffa2-5bdb-8d59-fab4fdcfe7bf" :answer-field/choices ["answer-690abb71-4d77-510e-a85b-3cd428ff1d32" "answer-a6b2db0b-70aa-51ed-a1c5-b48983c58ff7" "answer-8795d4c5-ffa2-5bdb-8d59-fab4fdcfe7bf" "answer-7f70533b-31ac-541b-be61-eba3464f5b2a" "answer-3eb3bff0-d429-5165-9782-7f65936a7d72"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-690abb71-4d77-510e-a85b-3cd428ff1d32" :answer/id #uuid "690abb71-4d77-510e-a85b-3cd428ff1d32" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a6b2db0b-70aa-51ed-a1c5-b48983c58ff7" :answer/id #uuid "a6b2db0b-70aa-51ed-a1c5-b48983c58ff7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8795d4c5-ffa2-5bdb-8d59-fab4fdcfe7bf" :answer/id #uuid "8795d4c5-ffa2-5bdb-8d59-fab4fdcfe7bf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f70533b-31ac-541b-be61-eba3464f5b2a" :answer/id #uuid "7f70533b-31ac-541b-be61-eba3464f5b2a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3eb3bff0-d429-5165-9782-7f65936a7d72" :answer/id #uuid "3eb3bff0-d429-5165-9782-7f65936a7d72" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-b940a17e-dd53-53d8-bf19-3b3ed1a866fe" :activity/id #uuid "b940a17e-dd53-53d8-bf19-3b3ed1a866fe" :activity/title "The Extreme Value Theorem" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048748 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-47653334-ddc8-59af-8924-8f0b31ddb36e" "step-fb835e05-9455-5b93-ad9d-42052fd2df0a" "step-0ea7f73f-9a2c-5b1b-92bd-1284bb0cf34e" "step-98d94b5f-1594-5a4d-b5ff-4c0b24e893f7" "step-bf9d0322-941e-5a54-b92e-f70ddfb5e71b"] :activity/first-step "step-47653334-ddc8-59af-8924-8f0b31ddb36e" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-47653334-ddc8-59af-8924-8f0b31ddb36e" :step/id #uuid "47653334-ddc8-59af-8924-8f0b31ddb36e" :step/content 17592186329840 :step/next "step-fb835e05-9455-5b93-ad9d-42052fd2df0a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-fb835e05-9455-5b93-ad9d-42052fd2df0a" :step/id #uuid "fb835e05-9455-5b93-ad9d-42052fd2df0a" :step/content 17592186341984 :step/next "step-0ea7f73f-9a2c-5b1b-92bd-1284bb0cf34e" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0ea7f73f-9a2c-5b1b-92bd-1284bb0cf34e" :step/id #uuid "0ea7f73f-9a2c-5b1b-92bd-1284bb0cf34e" :step/content 17592186341986 :step/next "step-98d94b5f-1594-5a4d-b5ff-4c0b24e893f7" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-98d94b5f-1594-5a4d-b5ff-4c0b24e893f7" :step/id #uuid "98d94b5f-1594-5a4d-b5ff-4c0b24e893f7" :step/content 17592186341993 :step/next "step-bf9d0322-941e-5a54-b92e-f70ddfb5e71b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-bf9d0322-941e-5a54-b92e-f70ddfb5e71b" :step/id #uuid "bf9d0322-941e-5a54-b92e-f70ddfb5e71b" :step/content "question-91c6ce97-3ba3-5af9-8b62-e9a8d179a50b" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229115 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e8cd627c-513a-5c63-8a84-0bf723703c1a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228888 :question/worked-solution "To find a number $30\\%$ *less than* $120,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 30\\% = 70\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n70\\% = \\dfrac{70}{100} = 0.7\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $70\\%$ of $120,$ we multiply $120$ by $0.7{:}$\n\n$$\n0.7 \\cdot 120 = 84\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229116 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d7719fa6-fde6-59d2-a1a3-a59122c8717b"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228878 :question/problem "A discount of $14\\%$ is offered on all hiking boots. What is the sale price of a pair of boots originally priced at $\\[math]78?$" :question/worked-solution "To find an amount that's $14\\%$ *less than* $\\[math]78,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 14\\% = 86\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n86\\% = \\dfrac{86}{100} = 0.86\n$$\n\nSo, to find $86\\%$ of $\\[/math]78,$ we multiply $\\[math]78$ by $0.86{:}$\n\n$$\n0.86 \\cdot \\[/math]78 = \\[math]67.08\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the hiking boots is $\\[/math]67.08.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229610 :question/problem "What number is $15%$ less than $80?$" :question/worked-solution "To find a number $15%$ *less than* $80,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-15%=85%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n85%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $85%$ of $80,$ we multiply $80$ by $0.85:$\n\n$$\n0.85\\cdot 80=68\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186227998 :answer-field/correct 17592186228000 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-e8cd627c-513a-5c63-8a84-0bf723703c1a" :question/id #uuid "e8cd627c-513a-5c63-8a84-0bf723703c1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-327179" :question/problem "What number is $15%$ less than $100$?" :question/worked-solution "To find a number $15%$ *less than* $100,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-15%=85%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n85%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $85%$ of $100,$ we multiply $100$ by $0.85:$\n\n$$\n0.85\\cdot 100=85\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0975c3e2-af49-5d0f-80bc-fa11172ade2c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0975c3e2-af49-5d0f-80bc-fa11172ade2c" :answer-field/id #uuid "0975c3e2-af49-5d0f-80bc-fa11172ade2c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-be33b89a-ee93-54fe-bdfa-8a5a33b86306" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3fea470f-479b-5c94-ab15-b4dc515b4071" "answer-20ca0958-9d8f-56d7-bd29-94a1848c1276" "answer-be33b89a-ee93-54fe-bdfa-8a5a33b86306" "answer-a790ee23-1330-55c3-bcb6-83fea352dc1d" "answer-f38458bf-a386-532e-86b7-47560d499b20"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fea470f-479b-5c94-ab15-b4dc515b4071" :answer/id #uuid "3fea470f-479b-5c94-ab15-b4dc515b4071" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "80" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-20ca0958-9d8f-56d7-bd29-94a1848c1276" :answer/id #uuid "20ca0958-9d8f-56d7-bd29-94a1848c1276" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "82" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be33b89a-ee93-54fe-bdfa-8a5a33b86306" :answer/id #uuid "be33b89a-ee93-54fe-bdfa-8a5a33b86306" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "85" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a790ee23-1330-55c3-bcb6-83fea352dc1d" :answer/id #uuid "a790ee23-1330-55c3-bcb6-83fea352dc1d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "88" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f38458bf-a386-532e-86b7-47560d499b20" :answer/id #uuid "f38458bf-a386-532e-86b7-47560d499b20" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "90" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d7719fa6-fde6-59d2-a1a3-a59122c8717b" :question/id #uuid "d7719fa6-fde6-59d2-a1a3-a59122c8717b" :question/math-academy-id "q-326544" :question/problem "An office chair is marked down by $15%$ during a promotion. What is the sale price of the chair if its original price is $$85?$" :question/worked-solution "To find an amount that's $15%$ *less than* $$85,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-15%=85%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n85%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85\n$$\n\nSo, to find $85%$ of $$85,$ we multiply $$85$ by $0.85:$\n\n$$\n0.85\\cdot $85=$72.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the chair is $$72.25.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c5312bb0-f9b0-5ad2-9bbb-517845ea0b67"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c5312bb0-f9b0-5ad2-9bbb-517845ea0b67" :answer-field/id #uuid "c5312bb0-f9b0-5ad2-9bbb-517845ea0b67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-a18f0694-e14d-5621-a0cd-e736598ab64d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5cfcbd7c-3cad-5dc2-9e79-693fbe4c4b98" "answer-0fc34084-dac6-528b-9bab-3c95b340ec89" "answer-a18f0694-e14d-5621-a0cd-e736598ab64d" "answer-18ab41f3-9e7c-549d-8e33-45a4d949c8b7" "answer-08ba13fe-1d0b-507b-85bb-128d69277956"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5cfcbd7c-3cad-5dc2-9e79-693fbe4c4b98" :answer/id #uuid "5cfcbd7c-3cad-5dc2-9e79-693fbe4c4b98" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$70.00$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0fc34084-dac6-528b-9bab-3c95b340ec89" :answer/id #uuid "0fc34084-dac6-528b-9bab-3c95b340ec89" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$71.50$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a18f0694-e14d-5621-a0cd-e736598ab64d" :answer/id #uuid "a18f0694-e14d-5621-a0cd-e736598ab64d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$72.25$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-18ab41f3-9e7c-549d-8e33-45a4d949c8b7" :answer/id #uuid "18ab41f3-9e7c-549d-8e33-45a4d949c8b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$73.00$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08ba13fe-1d0b-507b-85bb-128d69277956" :answer/id #uuid "08ba13fe-1d0b-507b-85bb-128d69277956" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$74.25$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229595 :question/problem "At a furniture store, all tables are discounted by $25%$ during a weekend sale. What is the sale price of a table that originally costs $$64?$" :question/worked-solution "To find an amount that's $25%$ *less than* $$64,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-25%=75%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n75%=\\frac{75}{100}=0.75\n$$\n\nSo, to find $75%$ of $$64,$ we multiply $$64$ by $0.75:$\n\n$$\n0.75\\cdot $64=$48\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the table is $$48.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186227913 :answer-field/correct 17592186227918 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186230172 :tutorial/content "In a previous lesson, we spent some time learning how to find a number that is a certain percentage *greater than* a given amount. This is known as finding a *percentage increase.*\n\nWe can apply similar reasoning to find a number that is a certain percentage *less than* a given amount. This corresponds to applying a **percentage decrease.**\n\nTo apply a percentage decrease, we do the following:\n\n1\\. Subtract the decrease from $100\\%.$\n\n2\\. Convert the result to a decimal multiplier.\n\n3\\. Find the product of the multiplier and the original number.\n\nLet's take a look at a worked example." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186230242 :tutorial/content "Consider the following problem.\n\nWhat number is $20\\%$ less than $250?$\n\nThe key words here are \"$20\\%$ *less than.*\" This means we need to apply a percentage decrease, decreasing the original number by $20\\%.$\n\nTo solve this problem, we follow three steps.\n\n- **Step 1.** Subtract the decrease from $100\\%.$\n\nTo find a number $20\\%$ *less than* $250,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% - 20\\% = 80\\%.\n$$\n\nThis means that our new number is $80\\%$ of $250.$\n\n- **Step 2.** Convert the result to a decimal multiplier.\n\nNow, we convert the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n80\\% = \\dfrac{80}{100} = 0.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Find the product of the multiplier and the original number.\n\nSo, to find a number that is $20\\%$ less than $250,$ we multiply $250$ by $0.8{:}$\n\n$$\n0.8 \\cdot 250 = 200\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $20\\%$ less than $250$ is $200.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186229244 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124765 :question/problem "Let $D$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ matrices of the form $[\\begin{aligned}a & 0 \\\\ 0 & a\\end{aligned}],$ where $a\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?  \n\nI. The identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]$ belongs to $D.$\n\nII. For the zero matrix $O$ and any $A,B\\in D,$ we have $A+O=O.$\n\nIII. For any $A,B\\in D,$ we have $A+B=B+A.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, every element $A$ in $D$ satisfies ${a}_{12}={a}_{21}=0$ and ${a}_{11}={a}_{22}.$ So, ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in D.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, consider the identity matrix ${I}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\in D.$ Then\n\n$$\n{I}_{2}+O=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\ne O.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, For any matrices $A,B\\in D,$ we obtain that \n\n$$\nA+B=B+A\n$$\n\nsince matrix addition is commutative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186121187 :answer-field/correct 17592186121190 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124589 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-e4e1e0d5-3773-5038-a8b4-577c1254dd92"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-e4e1e0d5-3773-5038-a8b4-577c1254dd92" :question/id #uuid "e4e1e0d5-3773-5038-a8b4-577c1254dd92" :question/math-academy-id "q-106807" :question/problem "Let $P$ be the set of all $2\\times 2$ real matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ such that ${a}_{22}=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. For any $A,B\\in P$ and any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we have $\\alpha (A+B)=\\alpha A+\\alpha B.$\n\nII. The scalar multiplication of matrices in $P$ is associative.\n\nIII. For any $A\\in P,$ we have $1\\cdot A={I}_{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's verify the statements one by one.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matrices $A=({a}_{ij})$ and $B=({b}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha ({a}_{ij}+{b}_{ij})=\\alpha {a}_{ij}+\\alpha {b}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (A+B)=\\alpha A+\\alpha B.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, for any $2\\times 2$ matric $A=({a}_{ij})$ from $P,$ the entries are just real numbers. Thus, for any $\\alpha ,\\beta \\in R,$ we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta {a}_{ij})=(\\alpha \\beta ){a}_{ij},\n$$\n\nand hence,\n\n$$\n\\alpha (\\beta A)=(\\alpha \\beta )A.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]$ from $P.$ Then,\n\n$$\n1\\cdot A=1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot 0 \\\\ 1\\cdot 1 & 1\\cdot 0\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\ne {I}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d4fdc251-4753-54c7-b5d7-bf5f38519bcc"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d4fdc251-4753-54c7-b5d7-bf5f38519bcc" :answer-field/id #uuid "d4fdc251-4753-54c7-b5d7-bf5f38519bcc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-23a1c3e8-9ac7-5fd0-b915-254166ffbcec" :answer-field/choices ["answer-23a1c3e8-9ac7-5fd0-b915-254166ffbcec" "answer-b86ca311-5ec4-5f1a-90f3-2144f5d7ec2c" "answer-cb2e1c85-661c-5c30-a579-2a791c364ae9" "answer-5e7e6612-1d6f-535f-8078-00cc2ee22135" "answer-b13597ee-dc96-5f12-bf58-96c7d9dbf7cd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23a1c3e8-9ac7-5fd0-b915-254166ffbcec" :answer/id #uuid "23a1c3e8-9ac7-5fd0-b915-254166ffbcec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b86ca311-5ec4-5f1a-90f3-2144f5d7ec2c" :answer/id #uuid "b86ca311-5ec4-5f1a-90f3-2144f5d7ec2c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cb2e1c85-661c-5c30-a579-2a791c364ae9" :answer/id #uuid "cb2e1c85-661c-5c30-a579-2a791c364ae9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e7e6612-1d6f-535f-8078-00cc2ee22135" :answer/id #uuid "5e7e6612-1d6f-535f-8078-00cc2ee22135" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b13597ee-dc96-5f12-bf58-96c7d9dbf7cd" :answer/id #uuid "b13597ee-dc96-5f12-bf58-96c7d9dbf7cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-2f4c21a1-9d38-5bd3-b500-c840f8fe443d" :activity/id #uuid "2f4c21a1-9d38-5bd3-b500-c840f8fe443d" :activity/title "Introduction to Abstract Vector Spaces" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049737 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-b1ceacff-cce5-592c-aeca-52a40311b2f1" "step-b3319bae-270f-56a4-8501-7d24a9dbec5a" "step-8061fe35-5bb2-5a7a-a40b-747237b37284" "step-6fe086d3-2ba5-5ffd-ac7b-c7f62891a1d0" "step-0c665127-012e-5722-82c0-c620231782dc"] :activity/first-step "step-b1ceacff-cce5-592c-aeca-52a40311b2f1" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-b1ceacff-cce5-592c-aeca-52a40311b2f1" :step/id #uuid "b1ceacff-cce5-592c-aeca-52a40311b2f1" :step/content 17592186360469 :step/next "step-b3319bae-270f-56a4-8501-7d24a9dbec5a" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b3319bae-270f-56a4-8501-7d24a9dbec5a" :step/id #uuid "b3319bae-270f-56a4-8501-7d24a9dbec5a" :step/content 17592186124765 :step/next "step-8061fe35-5bb2-5a7a-a40b-747237b37284" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-8061fe35-5bb2-5a7a-a40b-747237b37284" :step/id #uuid "8061fe35-5bb2-5a7a-a40b-747237b37284" :step/content "question-e4e1e0d5-3773-5038-a8b4-577c1254dd92" :step/next "step-6fe086d3-2ba5-5ffd-ac7b-c7f62891a1d0" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6fe086d3-2ba5-5ffd-ac7b-c7f62891a1d0" :step/id #uuid "6fe086d3-2ba5-5ffd-ac7b-c7f62891a1d0" :step/content 17592186281526 :step/next "step-0c665127-012e-5722-82c0-c620231782dc" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-0c665127-012e-5722-82c0-c620231782dc" :step/id #uuid "0c665127-012e-5722-82c0-c620231782dc" :step/content 17592186281527 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186104644 :answer-field/choices 17592186104645] [:db/retract 17592186104644 :answer-field/choices 17592186104646] [:db/retract 17592186104644 :answer-field/choices 17592186104647] [:db/retract 17592186104644 :answer-field/choices 17592186104648] [:db/retract 17592186104644 :answer-field/choices 17592186104649] {:db/id 17592186105930 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9d8646f3-f09f-5c98-8c8f-8b6c0f35dfe5" "question-bbbaba03-02d6-5c29-9b46-9da0f0be8cbb"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105534 :question/worked-solution "The area of a square with sides of length $a$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a^2.\n$$\n\nNote that each side of a grid cell measures $3\\,\\textrm{ft},$ and each side of the shaded square covers $4$ grid cells. So, the length of each side of the shaded square is\n\n$$\na = 3 \\times 4 = 12 \\, \\textrm{ft} .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the shaded square is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={a}^{2}={12}^{2}=144\\,{ft}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105932 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-da7691ce-ed2d-5165-8a4d-fdf1f9bd503b" "question-a60833b6-ee33-5277-9b1d-5d1fccd3856a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105537 :question/worked-solution "The area of a rectangle is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the sides.\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that \n\n$$\na = 4.5\\,\\mathrm{m}, \\qquad b = 6\\,\\mathrm{m}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the area of the hall is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b=4.5\\cdot 6=27\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105935 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6c1d9607-760c-5f72-b720-f7fb112fffe1"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105538 :question/problem "The area of the rectangular field shown below is $2\\,400\\,\\textrm{m}^2.$ What is the value of $x?$\n\n![](images/94/9419ae4a3099216109bff99906e5d1724a8f01f61e48e22edc62c0e1b62a1bef.png)" :question/worked-solution "The area of a rectangle is given by \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the sides.\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = 2\\,400\\mathrm{m}^2, \\qquad a = 60\\,\\mathrm{m}, \\qquad b=x .\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b \\\\ 2\\,400 & =60\\cdot x \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\,400}{60} \\\\ x & =40\\,\\text{m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105838 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-9fa4030e-f9e5-59f6-ba9d-65a980d49dfe" "question-404f0ec1-ce52-56bd-b28f-4f5a47723b39"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105671 :question/problem "Find the area of the rectangle $ABCD$ if its perimeter is $40$ and ${BC} = 12.$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the length of ${AB}$ by using the formula for the perimeter, as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+CD+DA \\\\ p & =2AB+2BC \\\\ 40 & =2AB+2(12) \\\\ 40 & =2AB+24 \\\\ 2AB & =16 \\\\ AB & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle $ABCD$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =8\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =96.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d8646f3-f09f-5c98-8c8f-8b6c0f35dfe5" :question/id #uuid "9d8646f3-f09f-5c98-8c8f-8b6c0f35dfe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-117179" :question/problem "![](images/e0/e0a9d0aeba77f3ca2cd472b9c65df156394990a89a643e9a316ee662a52eb6b3.png)\n\nDavid wants to redesign his house, changing a small window for a big square window. The dimensions of the square window are shown in the figure above. What area covers the window?" :question/worked-solution "The area of a square with sides of length $a$ is given by \n\n$$\nA={a}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that $a=4\\,m.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={a}^{2}={4}^{2}=16\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f89de56d-ac6c-534d-88b4-b655b72b78ce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f89de56d-ac6c-534d-88b4-b655b72b78ce" :answer-field/id #uuid "f89de56d-ac6c-534d-88b4-b655b72b78ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1a60e3b4-8113-5635-b39f-7eaebb4ff363" :answer-field/choices ["answer-03de657b-46bc-5dde-8c43-330d74e9b648" "answer-1a60e3b4-8113-5635-b39f-7eaebb4ff363" "answer-dd31e868-7aca-569d-85dd-2555e24bdd5a" "answer-8c72d778-672c-59f3-8acb-ac99286d54b8" "answer-1456890e-75d0-549d-8079-a328093ce956"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03de657b-46bc-5dde-8c43-330d74e9b648" :answer/id #uuid "03de657b-46bc-5dde-8c43-330d74e9b648" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a60e3b4-8113-5635-b39f-7eaebb4ff363" :answer/id #uuid "1a60e3b4-8113-5635-b39f-7eaebb4ff363" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd31e868-7aca-569d-85dd-2555e24bdd5a" :answer/id #uuid "dd31e868-7aca-569d-85dd-2555e24bdd5a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c72d778-672c-59f3-8acb-ac99286d54b8" :answer/id #uuid "8c72d778-672c-59f3-8acb-ac99286d54b8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1456890e-75d0-549d-8079-a328093ce956" :answer/id #uuid "1456890e-75d0-549d-8079-a328093ce956" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bbbaba03-02d6-5c29-9b46-9da0f0be8cbb" :question/id #uuid "bbbaba03-02d6-5c29-9b46-9da0f0be8cbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-117181" :question/problem "![](images/c3/c3f298b4df4e0c8ff66ad9da970c16ab11839f014519fd451dfba050500d9513.png)\n\nA family is having a picnic in a park. They place a big square blanket on the ground. The dimensions of the blanket are shown in the figure above. What is the area covered by the blanket?" :question/worked-solution "The area of a square with sides of length $a$ is given by \n\n$$\nA={a}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that $a=7\\,ft.$ \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={a}^{2}={7}^{2}=49\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-efc75398-cc75-59e2-8528-2b244f9d9f26"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-efc75398-cc75-59e2-8528-2b244f9d9f26" :answer-field/id #uuid "efc75398-cc75-59e2-8528-2b244f9d9f26" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3c720ea8-dccc-543a-aeb3-89a2e236ca67" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4115a1b9-36b7-524d-9e1b-d151a5cbc361" "answer-c66c5919-00de-52e0-8e67-17e321393e5f" "answer-3c720ea8-dccc-543a-aeb3-89a2e236ca67" "answer-578b0918-88d0-55e9-8da6-226d9d8b7b75" "answer-7b17b055-bee6-5e61-a432-b94d1d81ecdc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4115a1b9-36b7-524d-9e1b-d151a5cbc361" :answer/id #uuid "4115a1b9-36b7-524d-9e1b-d151a5cbc361" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c66c5919-00de-52e0-8e67-17e321393e5f" :answer/id #uuid "c66c5919-00de-52e0-8e67-17e321393e5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c720ea8-dccc-543a-aeb3-89a2e236ca67" :answer/id #uuid "3c720ea8-dccc-543a-aeb3-89a2e236ca67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "49\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-578b0918-88d0-55e9-8da6-226d9d8b7b75" :answer/id #uuid "578b0918-88d0-55e9-8da6-226d9d8b7b75" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "60\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7b17b055-bee6-5e61-a432-b94d1d81ecdc" :answer/id #uuid "7b17b055-bee6-5e61-a432-b94d1d81ecdc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-da7691ce-ed2d-5165-8a4d-fdf1f9bd503b" :question/id #uuid "da7691ce-ed2d-5165-8a4d-fdf1f9bd503b" :question/math-academy-id "q-53230" :question/problem "![](images/47/473b8a18bb4d2bf77cfe18eb735bcb64ec95a551faadb18ad38f561c81d3b99c.png)\n\nFind the area of the rectangle $ABCD$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "The area of a rectangle is given by \n\n$$\nA=a\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the sides.\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that \n\n$$\na=AB=9\\,cm,\\,b=AD=5\\,cm.\n$$\n\n  \n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b=9\\cdot 5=45\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fc90adbc-d567-5552-83aa-263b9e6bc19f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fc90adbc-d567-5552-83aa-263b9e6bc19f" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc90adbc-d567-5552-83aa-263b9e6bc19f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c1e70cc4-b54b-5060-9470-1102d68bc62d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4680e928-ba75-5e32-b8a7-6fc72e31205d" "answer-aadcf2f2-5ca0-5416-90d4-f9a76cd9413a" "answer-f5b5c4b1-092b-5ffc-b03e-7f00c447bda0" "answer-c1e70cc4-b54b-5060-9470-1102d68bc62d" "answer-e1c4ff5e-1dad-5365-9d58-80e9b4d5e154"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4680e928-ba75-5e32-b8a7-6fc72e31205d" :answer/id #uuid "4680e928-ba75-5e32-b8a7-6fc72e31205d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1.5\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aadcf2f2-5ca0-5416-90d4-f9a76cd9413a" :answer/id #uuid "aadcf2f2-5ca0-5416-90d4-f9a76cd9413a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f5b5c4b1-092b-5ffc-b03e-7f00c447bda0" :answer/id #uuid "f5b5c4b1-092b-5ffc-b03e-7f00c447bda0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1e70cc4-b54b-5060-9470-1102d68bc62d" :answer/id #uuid "c1e70cc4-b54b-5060-9470-1102d68bc62d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "45\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e1c4ff5e-1dad-5365-9d58-80e9b4d5e154" :answer/id #uuid "e1c4ff5e-1dad-5365-9d58-80e9b4d5e154" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "81\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a60833b6-ee33-5277-9b1d-5d1fccd3856a" :question/id #uuid "a60833b6-ee33-5277-9b1d-5d1fccd3856a" :question/math-academy-id "q-117186" :question/problem "![](images/eb/ebb60aa69c324ef1bfc70bc325ff71eca70426225859350c4bbd8a58a82a2757.png)\n\nThe parking lot in a market has a rectangular form whose dimensions are shown in the figure above. What is the area of the parking lot?" :question/worked-solution "The area of a rectangle is given by \n\n$$\nA=a\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the sides.\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that \n\n$$\na=200\\,m,\\,b=60\\,m.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,  the area of the field is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b=200\\cdot 60=12\\,000\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5e4da27b-2dce-5bd1-a672-f45817b5055e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5e4da27b-2dce-5bd1-a672-f45817b5055e" :answer-field/id #uuid "5e4da27b-2dce-5bd1-a672-f45817b5055e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-75e6271a-2f89-50d8-93ce-5d808e712bbc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bb4ec6fa-3905-5dc7-ac24-fccd33798378" "answer-41151962-0dd9-572c-96e9-fc4feef8073d" "answer-75e6271a-2f89-50d8-93ce-5d808e712bbc" "answer-4b9b7239-8ac1-5cee-bf69-99f6fbd3fc48" "answer-664ce2e7-5431-5dd8-bd05-3a8b7d8e846d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb4ec6fa-3905-5dc7-ac24-fccd33798378" :answer/id #uuid "bb4ec6fa-3905-5dc7-ac24-fccd33798378" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10\\,000\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41151962-0dd9-572c-96e9-fc4feef8073d" :answer/id #uuid "41151962-0dd9-572c-96e9-fc4feef8073d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11\\,000\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75e6271a-2f89-50d8-93ce-5d808e712bbc" :answer/id #uuid "75e6271a-2f89-50d8-93ce-5d808e712bbc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "12\\,000\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4b9b7239-8ac1-5cee-bf69-99f6fbd3fc48" :answer/id #uuid "4b9b7239-8ac1-5cee-bf69-99f6fbd3fc48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "13\\,000\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-664ce2e7-5431-5dd8-bd05-3a8b7d8e846d" :answer/id #uuid "664ce2e7-5431-5dd8-bd05-3a8b7d8e846d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9\\,000\\,{\\text{m}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106416 :question/problem "![](images/91/91a554b467201b7507b531dcc0b6e1d5d9970d3e6d6554878644a31e5e38d9fa.png)\n\nThe area of the rectangular field shown above is $500\\,{\\text{yd}}^{2}.$ What is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "The area of a rectangle is given by \n\n$$\nA=a\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the sides.\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that \n\n$$\na=20\\,yd,\\,A=500\\,{yd}^{2},\\,b=x.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b \\\\ 500 & =20\\cdot x \\\\ x & =\\frac{500}{20} \\\\ x & =25\\,\\text{yd}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186104644 :answer-field/correct "answer-985d6f45-92ac-5d55-8b62-94279162d932" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0f0416e4-9fea-506b-aeca-f53c05795336" "answer-ea757878-f4e8-5eaf-8d63-8a16527b1941" "answer-985d6f45-92ac-5d55-8b62-94279162d932" "answer-f4f5e77b-84f1-5eb3-b451-184e0ab46010" "answer-8752afd1-f7b3-5a00-a5f6-d480894af162"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f0416e4-9fea-506b-aeca-f53c05795336" :answer/id #uuid "0f0416e4-9fea-506b-aeca-f53c05795336" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "15\\,{\\text{yd}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea757878-f4e8-5eaf-8d63-8a16527b1941" :answer/id #uuid "ea757878-f4e8-5eaf-8d63-8a16527b1941" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20\\,\\text{yd}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-985d6f45-92ac-5d55-8b62-94279162d932" :answer/id #uuid "985d6f45-92ac-5d55-8b62-94279162d932" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25\\,\\text{yd}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f4f5e77b-84f1-5eb3-b451-184e0ab46010" :answer/id #uuid "f4f5e77b-84f1-5eb3-b451-184e0ab46010" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30\\,\\text{yd}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8752afd1-f7b3-5a00-a5f6-d480894af162" :answer/id #uuid "8752afd1-f7b3-5a00-a5f6-d480894af162" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "500\\,\\text{yd}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6c1d9607-760c-5f72-b720-f7fb112fffe1" :question/id #uuid "6c1d9607-760c-5f72-b720-f7fb112fffe1" :question/math-academy-id "q-53279" :question/problem "![](images/54/547debca3e0215ebad8db2c525f9d1b0d435379c081073013cdbfd52b1b6c0f9.png)\n\nThe area of the rectangular court shown above is $56\\,{\\text{yd}}^{2}.$ What is the value of $x?$" :question/worked-solution "The area of a rectangle is given by \n\n$$\nA=a\\cdot b,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the lengths of the sides.\n\nFrom the figure, we obtain that \n\n$$\na=2x-6\\,yd,\\,A=56\\,{yd}^{2},\\,b=7\\,\\text{yd}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b \\\\ 56 & =(2x-6)\\cdot 7 \\\\ 56 & =14x-42 \\\\ 98 & =14x \\\\ x & =7\\,yd.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a85c8dd0-4a9d-5964-a329-cd025d7bcde3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a85c8dd0-4a9d-5964-a329-cd025d7bcde3" :answer-field/id #uuid "a85c8dd0-4a9d-5964-a329-cd025d7bcde3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b69cffb8-30df-591a-bace-f9c4e3d14f00" :answer-field/choices ["answer-0d1b9500-8c1e-52f2-af98-5cf3b4505950" "answer-415830b2-4096-54b1-a1e0-46e1dfd68021" "answer-b5823cf8-062c-5722-8501-a426a438dd0a" "answer-b69cffb8-30df-591a-bace-f9c4e3d14f00" "answer-f47bdc75-a728-5b31-bfab-d252c6171250"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d1b9500-8c1e-52f2-af98-5cf3b4505950" :answer/id #uuid "0d1b9500-8c1e-52f2-af98-5cf3b4505950" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3\\,yd" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-415830b2-4096-54b1-a1e0-46e1dfd68021" :answer/id #uuid "415830b2-4096-54b1-a1e0-46e1dfd68021" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,yd" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5823cf8-062c-5722-8501-a426a438dd0a" :answer/id #uuid "b5823cf8-062c-5722-8501-a426a438dd0a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6\\,yd" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b69cffb8-30df-591a-bace-f9c4e3d14f00" :answer/id #uuid "b69cffb8-30df-591a-bace-f9c4e3d14f00" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "7\\,yd" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f47bdc75-a728-5b31-bfab-d252c6171250" :answer/id #uuid "f47bdc75-a728-5b31-bfab-d252c6171250" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8\\,yd" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9fa4030e-f9e5-59f6-ba9d-65a980d49dfe" :question/id #uuid "9fa4030e-f9e5-59f6-ba9d-65a980d49dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-117071" :question/problem "Find the area of the rectangle $ABCD$ if its perimeter is $36$ and $BC=10.$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the length of $AB$ by using the formula for the perimeter, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+CD+DA \\\\ p & =2AB+2BC \\\\ 36 & =2AB+2(10) \\\\ 36 & =2AB+20 \\\\ 2AB & =16 \\\\ AB & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the rectangle $ABCD$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =10\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e48cd3c9-0b59-5bac-b974-bed484653ace"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e48cd3c9-0b59-5bac-b974-bed484653ace" :answer-field/id #uuid "e48cd3c9-0b59-5bac-b974-bed484653ace" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cbe3798c-bab3-5379-b806-51f51ae37774" :answer-field/choices ["answer-147d44b6-be70-5985-87d2-359212c64abf" "answer-cbe3798c-bab3-5379-b806-51f51ae37774" "answer-86e179f4-f4b1-5e05-a5f4-196353e934d4" "answer-e12b6b48-1bc0-5cef-872e-4ec7e81b37b2" "answer-4ba6dc5d-2df7-5ff1-9d5c-1e987f6e3b36"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-147d44b6-be70-5985-87d2-359212c64abf" :answer/id #uuid "147d44b6-be70-5985-87d2-359212c64abf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "70" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cbe3798c-bab3-5379-b806-51f51ae37774" :answer/id #uuid "cbe3798c-bab3-5379-b806-51f51ae37774" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "80" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-86e179f4-f4b1-5e05-a5f4-196353e934d4" :answer/id #uuid "86e179f4-f4b1-5e05-a5f4-196353e934d4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "85" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e12b6b48-1bc0-5cef-872e-4ec7e81b37b2" :answer/id #uuid "e12b6b48-1bc0-5cef-872e-4ec7e81b37b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "90" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ba6dc5d-2df7-5ff1-9d5c-1e987f6e3b36" :answer/id #uuid "4ba6dc5d-2df7-5ff1-9d5c-1e987f6e3b36" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "92" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-404f0ec1-ce52-56bd-b28f-4f5a47723b39" :question/id #uuid "404f0ec1-ce52-56bd-b28f-4f5a47723b39" :question/math-academy-id "q-171945" :question/problem "The perimeter of the rectangle $ABCD$ is $32.$ If $AB=3BC,$ then its area is $A=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $BC=x.$\n\nSince the perimeter of the rectangle is $p=32$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =AB+BC+CD+DA \\\\ p & =2(AB)+2(BC) \\\\ 32 & =2(3x)+2(x) \\\\ 32 & =6x+2x \\\\ 8x & =32 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we obtain $BC=4$ and $AB=3\\cdot 4=12.$\n\nTherefore, the area of a rectangle $ABCD$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =12\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-7cdfb13a-f513-5cd8-ae5d-a5e74bfaf7ee"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-7cdfb13a-f513-5cd8-ae5d-a5e74bfaf7ee" :answer-field/id #uuid "7cdfb13a-f513-5cd8-ae5d-a5e74bfaf7ee" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-538c3464-d6d4-5422-a36d-0a0043f95251" :answer-field/choices ["answer-538c3464-d6d4-5422-a36d-0a0043f95251"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-538c3464-d6d4-5422-a36d-0a0043f95251" :answer/id #uuid "538c3464-d6d4-5422-a36d-0a0043f95251" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107049 :tutorial/content "The **area** of a shape is a measure of how much space is enclosed by a shape on a plane. Let's consider a square with sides of length of $1\\,\\textrm{in}.$\n\n![](images/bc/bc59b6e5a60a2f22e6c7fb102eba2f255dd09e115fd04a16050570f9f8b053f4.png)\n\nThe area of this square is equal to ${\\color{red}{1\\,\\textrm{in}^2}},$ which we say in words as \"one square inch.\"\n\nSimilarly, if we have three squares with sides of length $1\\,\\textrm{cm},$ $1\\,\\textrm{m},$ and $1\\,\\textrm{ft}$ respectively, then their respective areas will be \n\n$$\n1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2, \\qquad\n\t1\\,\\textrm{m}^2, \\qquad \n\t1\\,\\textrm{ft}^2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107050 :tutorial/content "Let's now suppose that we would like to compute the area of the square $ABCD,$ shown below. Notice that, this time, the sides of the square span multiple unit lengths.\n\n![](images/14/1441893036f9a41ed0daa8c8ce078d6a733bab5bb88dc60259a6b9ebc1c2e7c0.png)\n\nThe length of one side of a grid square is equal to $1\\,\\textrm{cm}.$ So, the area of one grid square is $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2.$ \n\nNow, we need to find how many grid squares are contained inside $ABCD.$\n\n![](images/5f/5f293762b05013689996298a7804fb5f507eb228112397641fb8f662ff74ba49.png)\n\nInside the large blue square, there are $3$ rows containing $3$ grid squares each, so we can fit exactly $3 \\times 3 = 3^2 = 9$ grid squares inside $ABCD.$\n\nEach grid square has area $1\\,\\textrm{cm}^2,$ so the area $\\mathcal A$ of $ABCD$ is \n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = (3\\,\\textrm{cm})^2 = 9\\,\\textrm{cm}^2.\n$$\n\nIn general, if we have a square with a side of length $a$, then the area of the square is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a^2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186107052 :tutorial/content "In general, for a rectangle with sides of length $a$ and $b,$ the area of the rectangle is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = a \\cdot b.\n$$\n\nLet's demonstrate by finding the area of the rectangle $PQRS,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/a0/a0890441b4c7e888ca7ede71a075825ca6700f79d242d1dec7e35ab2d787e5e0.png)\n\nFrom the figure, we see that the rectangle has the following side lengths:\n\n$$\na = {PQ} = 10\\,\\mathrm{cm},\n\t\\qquad\n\tb = {PS} = 6\\,\\mathrm{cm}.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{cm}\\cdot 6\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =60\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIt does not matter which side we call $a$ and which side we call $b.$ In the above example, if we set\n\n$$\na = {QR} = 6\\,\\mathrm{cm},\n\\qquad\nb = {RS} = 10\\,\\mathrm{cm},\n$$\n\n \n\nthen we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =a\\cdot b \\\\  & =10\\,\\text{cm}\\cdot 6\\,\\text{cm} \\\\  & =60\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas before." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105987 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100836 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d85466ba-cd37-5739-bfc9-c486bbfacc9a" "question-9284b07a-9db4-5c4c-b135-110855ca0f14" "question-06dbc133-9492-57ef-8ac9-643d61a66e1b" "question-6e590d69-9e57-5a7b-a44f-da12f0280772" "question-500619bc-56ac-575b-907b-2407a9ecc2e7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100646 :question/problem "Suppose that we have a string of length $20$ inches and that we shape it to make a circular sector, as shown in the diagram below.  Among all of the circular sectors that can be made with that string, what's the radius of the one that gives the largest area?\n\n![](images/04/0473dd96f4f5f27c03d0ecbbba7ed0c0458527e71f515e242ec790d1dfea2d09.png)" :question/worked-solution "First,  let $r$ stand for the circular sector's radius and let $\\theta$ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA = \\dfrac{1}{2}r^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter of our sector is fixed at $20$ inches in length, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n20=2r + r\\theta,\n$$\n\nwhere we recall that $s=r\\theta$ gives the arc-length $s$ of a circular sector of radius $r$ and central angle $\\theta,$ where $\\theta$ is in radians.\n\nNow, by making $\\theta$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\theta = \\dfrac{20-2r}{r} = \\dfrac{20}{r} -2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\nA(r) &=  \\dfrac{1}{2}r^2\\left(\\dfrac{20}{r} -2\\right)\\[3pt]\n&= 10r -r^2.}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\nA'(r) = 10 -2r.\n$$\n\nSetting $A'(r) = 0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 10-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=5$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative is\n\n$$\nA''(r) = -2 \\quad \\Longrightarrow \\quad A''(5) = -2 < 0.\n$$\n\nSince $A''(5)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=5$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nTherefore, the circular sector of the greatest area will have a radius of $5\\,\\textrm{in},$ and the maximum area is given by\n\n$$\nA(5) = 10(5) - 5^2 = 25\\,\\textrm{in}^2.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100837 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-4d662c12-9d76-5b11-86a1-8fd56f6d8729" "question-92add898-4b49-5d01-bf37-847e609cbb26" "question-b59b20a8-8e7c-533f-bc4e-e028196934bf" "question-3990dbc6-49ea-5002-bf6b-028dcf68d1bf" "question-7c9b4d08-b984-5348-9fbd-0d702cd0c142"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186100647 :question/worked-solution "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta$ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP= 2r + r\\theta.\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $8$ square units,  so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n8 = \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that\n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n8 &= \\frac{1}{2}r^2\\theta\\[3pt]\n\\theta &= \\dfrac{16}{r^2}.\n}\n$$\n\nPlugging this value into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2r+r\\cdot \\frac{16}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =2r+\\frac{16}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\nP'(r) = 2 - \\dfrac{16}{r^2}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =16 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =8 \\\\ r & =2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=2\\sqrt{2}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative $P''(r) = \\dfrac{32}{r^3} > 0$, the stationary point $r=2\\sqrt{2}$ is a minimum. \n\nFinally, when $r=2\\sqrt{2}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{(2\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that the angle $\\theta = 2$ radians minimizes the perimeter of the circular sector $MON.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-d85466ba-cd37-5739-bfc9-c486bbfacc9a" :question/id #uuid "d85466ba-cd37-5739-bfc9-c486bbfacc9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-17957" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0da2ed0bca11725e7ffb3fa3c5952807e406530a0625b01ebb476f0145b64e30.png)\n\nA farmer wants to fence a field that is made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. He has only $200\\,m$ of fencing. What is the length $2x$ of the side of the field that covers the greatest area?" :question/worked-solution "First,  let $r$ the radius of the upper semicircle (top) and $x$ the radius of the lateral semicircle (right), hence the dimensions of the rectangle are $2r$ and $2x.$ We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =(2r)(2x)+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $200$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n200=\\pi r+\\pi x+2x+2r\\,.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}200 & =\\pi r+\\pi x+2x+2r \\\\ x(\\pi +2) & =200-r(\\pi +2) \\\\ x & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =4rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi {x}^{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4r(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}{(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)}^{2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =4(\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)+4r(-1)+\\pi r+\\pi (\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-r)(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{800}{\\pi +2}-4r-4r+\\pi r-\\frac{200\\pi }{\\pi +2}+\\pi r \\\\  & =-8r+2\\pi r+\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ -8r+2\\pi r+\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2} & =0 \\\\ 2r(\\pi -4) & =-\\frac{800-200\\pi }{\\pi +2} \\\\ 2r(\\pi -4) & =\\frac{200\\pi -800}{\\pi +2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100\\pi -400}{(\\pi +2)(\\pi -4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100(\\pi -4)}{(\\pi +2)(\\pi -4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-8+2\\pi \\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{100}{\\pi +2})=-8+2\\pi <0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{100}{\\pi +2})$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}-\\frac{100}{\\pi +2} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{\\pi +2}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the side of the field with the largest area is $2x=\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-18d6be88-7869-5ea2-874d-dbe10543c41d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-18d6be88-7869-5ea2-874d-dbe10543c41d" :answer-field/id #uuid "18d6be88-7869-5ea2-874d-dbe10543c41d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-5acae424-da37-50ed-b389-f23ad0386052" :answer-field/choices ["answer-5e0ef677-0dfb-56b7-b72e-a9eb57ef63cd" "answer-5acae424-da37-50ed-b389-f23ad0386052" "answer-2d1c04e1-50cd-5cdc-bde9-38e71d892e85" "answer-e79c2f39-3294-5d2a-83ce-47805402dd2e" "answer-136d2a61-b0f7-5dd5-8838-447c8e8d76e4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e0ef677-0dfb-56b7-b72e-a9eb57ef63cd" :answer/id #uuid "5e0ef677-0dfb-56b7-b72e-a9eb57ef63cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{100}{\\pi +4}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5acae424-da37-50ed-b389-f23ad0386052" :answer/id #uuid "5acae424-da37-50ed-b389-f23ad0386052" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{200}{\\pi +2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d1c04e1-50cd-5cdc-bde9-38e71d892e85" :answer/id #uuid "2d1c04e1-50cd-5cdc-bde9-38e71d892e85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{200}{\\pi +4}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e79c2f39-3294-5d2a-83ce-47805402dd2e" :answer/id #uuid "e79c2f39-3294-5d2a-83ce-47805402dd2e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{300}{\\pi +2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-136d2a61-b0f7-5dd5-8838-447c8e8d76e4" :answer/id #uuid "136d2a61-b0f7-5dd5-8838-447c8e8d76e4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{400}{\\pi +2}\\,\\text{m}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9284b07a-9db4-5c4c-b135-110855ca0f14" :question/id #uuid "9284b07a-9db4-5c4c-b135-110855ca0f14" :question/math-academy-id "q-344681" :question/problem "![](images/f1/f17ce467f5bff34f0c04326d1a23ca8621f459571be8c98a745174a3d5587337.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $MON$ with perimeter $P=100$ units. Determine the angle $\\theta $ (in radians) of the circular sector with the largest possible area. Do not round the answer.\n\n$\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}\nradians" :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $100$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n100=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{100-2r}{r}=\\frac{100}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{100}{r}-2) \\\\  & =50r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A$, so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=50-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 50-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=25$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(25)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(25)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=25$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=25$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\frac{100}{r}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{25}-2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f6880c81-e1e8-5f43-89ee-3d61afafa579"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f6880c81-e1e8-5f43-89ee-3d61afafa579" :answer-field/id #uuid "f6880c81-e1e8-5f43-89ee-3d61afafa579" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-fe0f3a0e-95e4-5ef5-9aec-3c4c7af2e50b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fe0f3a0e-95e4-5ef5-9aec-3c4c7af2e50b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fe0f3a0e-95e4-5ef5-9aec-3c4c7af2e50b" :answer/id #uuid "fe0f3a0e-95e4-5ef5-9aec-3c4c7af2e50b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-06dbc133-9492-57ef-8ac9-643d61a66e1b" :question/id #uuid "06dbc133-9492-57ef-8ac9-643d61a66e1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-344853" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9ea7b23d8dd14a0ed092152592800022400ee73e7b371146f5e972900f2c2f29.png)\n\nA community organizer wants to install a border around a walkway made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. She has only $120\\,m$ of border material. The organizer wishes to maximize the area of the **rectangle**. Find $x+2r$ where $x$ and $2r$ are the dimensions of the rectangle that has the greatest area. Do not round the answer.\n\n$x+2r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{m}$" :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius of the semicircles (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the length of the other side of the rectangle. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2rx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $120\\text{ m},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n120=\\pi r+\\pi r+2x=2\\pi r+2x.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120 & =2\\pi r+2x \\\\ 2x & =120-2\\pi r \\\\ x & =60-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =2r(60-\\pi r) \\\\  & =120r-2\\pi {r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =120-4\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 120-4\\pi r & =0 \\\\ 4\\pi r & =120 \\\\ r & =\\frac{30}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-4\\pi \\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(\\frac{30}{\\pi })=-4\\pi <0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(\\frac{30}{\\pi })$ is negative, we conclude that $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nFinally, when $r=\\frac{30}{\\pi }$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =60-\\pi (\\frac{30}{\\pi }) \\\\  & =60-30 \\\\  & =30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the dimensions of the rectangle with the largest area are $x=30$ and $2r=\\frac{60}{\\pi },$ and we conclude that\n\n$$\nx+2r=30+\\frac{60}{\\pi }=\\frac{30\\pi +60}{\\pi }.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bcf6bf10-deb1-51fd-ba83-03a05512720e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bcf6bf10-deb1-51fd-ba83-03a05512720e" :answer-field/id #uuid "bcf6bf10-deb1-51fd-ba83-03a05512720e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-b41a9298-fc3d-590a-8e9f-31f92e51c962" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b41a9298-fc3d-590a-8e9f-31f92e51c962"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b41a9298-fc3d-590a-8e9f-31f92e51c962" :answer/id #uuid "b41a9298-fc3d-590a-8e9f-31f92e51c962" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30+\\frac{60}{\\pi}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-6e590d69-9e57-5a7b-a44f-da12f0280772" :question/id #uuid "6e590d69-9e57-5a7b-a44f-da12f0280772" :question/math-academy-id "q-344791" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5cd71fa90a1213292961960facf049a1333beb63abeb53f08ba18173281fb330.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $ABC$ with perimeter $P=80$ units. Determine the largest possible area of such a circular sector. Do not round the answer.\n\nThe maximum area is\n{{field-1}}\nsquare units." :question/worked-solution "First, let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $80$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n80=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{80-2r}{r}=\\frac{80}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{80}{r}-2) \\\\  & =40r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A$, so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=40-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 40-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we confirm that $r=20$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(20)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(20)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=20$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$\n\nTherefore, the circular sector of the largest area will have a radius of $20,$ and the area is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =40r-{r}^{2} \\\\  & =40(20)-(20)^{2} \\\\  & =400.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-975fd3e3-b86b-5c26-9e9b-ffa0d6d69891"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-975fd3e3-b86b-5c26-9e9b-ffa0d6d69891" :answer-field/id #uuid "975fd3e3-b86b-5c26-9e9b-ffa0d6d69891" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-d68cfadd-4957-55c7-b502-d28278bf4278" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d68cfadd-4957-55c7-b502-d28278bf4278"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d68cfadd-4957-55c7-b502-d28278bf4278" :answer/id #uuid "d68cfadd-4957-55c7-b502-d28278bf4278" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "400" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-500619bc-56ac-575b-907b-2407a9ecc2e7" :question/id #uuid "500619bc-56ac-575b-907b-2407a9ecc2e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-51205" :question/problem "![](images/59/59949e4ba7818ea08d4613fee703a2c973ffa4953c820f04c1ae9279147dad56.png)\n\nConsider all possible circular sectors $MON$ with perimeter $P=180$ units. What is the radius $r$ of the circular sector with the largest possible area?" :question/worked-solution "First,  let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. We want to maximize the area\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the perimeter is fixed at $180$ units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n180=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation, we find that \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{180-2r}{r}=\\frac{180}{r}-2.\n$$\n\nPlugging the above into our expression for $A,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A(r) & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}(\\frac{180}{r}-2) \\\\  & =90r-{r}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to maximize $A,$ so we need to differentiate $A$ and set the derivative equal to zero. The derivative is \n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(r)=90-2r.\n$$\n\nSetting ${A}^{′}(r)=0$ and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(r) & =0 \\\\ 90-2r & =0 \\\\ r & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=45$ is a maximum using the second derivative test. The second derivative gives\n\n$$\n{A}^{″}(r)=-2\\,⟹\\,{A}^{″}(45)=-2<0.\n$$\n\nSince ${A}^{″}(45)$ is negative, we conclude that $r=45$ is indeed a maximum of $A(r).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1dc0977c-f93c-51ec-b77b-0003eb68d4f5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1dc0977c-f93c-51ec-b77b-0003eb68d4f5" :answer-field/id #uuid "1dc0977c-f93c-51ec-b77b-0003eb68d4f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-928c2847-7ef6-5e6c-9e86-4bdc08839ae1" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ce2951d6-7d10-5d24-95a2-411a03c4a3c4" "answer-c02779ce-52f0-516c-8624-96b7ce026a49" "answer-928c2847-7ef6-5e6c-9e86-4bdc08839ae1" "answer-fee47921-86a7-5e1e-846d-a82ad8a8f292" "answer-c928eb10-8758-5697-8bc6-529533e2ea40"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce2951d6-7d10-5d24-95a2-411a03c4a3c4" :answer/id #uuid "ce2951d6-7d10-5d24-95a2-411a03c4a3c4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$35$ units" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c02779ce-52f0-516c-8624-96b7ce026a49" :answer/id #uuid "c02779ce-52f0-516c-8624-96b7ce026a49" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$44.5$ units" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-928c2847-7ef6-5e6c-9e86-4bdc08839ae1" :answer/id #uuid "928c2847-7ef6-5e6c-9e86-4bdc08839ae1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$45$ units" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fee47921-86a7-5e1e-846d-a82ad8a8f292" :answer/id #uuid "fee47921-86a7-5e1e-846d-a82ad8a8f292" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$54$ units" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c928eb10-8758-5697-8bc6-529533e2ea40" :answer/id #uuid "c928eb10-8758-5697-8bc6-529533e2ea40" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$58$ units" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-4d662c12-9d76-5b11-86a1-8fd56f6d8729" :question/id #uuid "4d662c12-9d76-5b11-86a1-8fd56f6d8729" :question/math-academy-id "q-344938" :question/problem "![](images/60/60e04faa7448d351706ee1af70a8620590ca16e0debce7dd2e58631462594132.png)\n\nConsider all circular sectors $DEF$ with area $A=144$ square units. Determine the smallest possible perimeter $P$ of such a circular sector. Do not round the answer.\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}\nunits" :question/worked-solution "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $144$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n144={r}^{2}\\frac{\\theta }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}144 & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2r+r\\cdot \\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =2r+\\frac{288}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=2-\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =288 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =144 \\\\ r & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=12$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{576}{{r}^{3}}>0$, the stationary point $r=12$ is a minimum.\n\nFinally, when $r=12$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{288}{144} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dimensions of the circular sector with the smallest perimeter are $r=12$ and $\\theta =2.$ Therefore, the smallest perimeter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot 12+12\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24+24 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8f1618c4-780a-57b4-9a0f-35ba97b75da4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8f1618c4-780a-57b4-9a0f-35ba97b75da4" :answer-field/id #uuid "8f1618c4-780a-57b4-9a0f-35ba97b75da4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-bc531b63-ca0c-5196-ac8d-68aa088deb43" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc531b63-ca0c-5196-ac8d-68aa088deb43"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc531b63-ca0c-5196-ac8d-68aa088deb43" :answer/id #uuid "bc531b63-ca0c-5196-ac8d-68aa088deb43" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "48" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-92add898-4b49-5d01-bf37-847e609cbb26" :question/id #uuid "92add898-4b49-5d01-bf37-847e609cbb26" :question/math-academy-id "q-15089" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9b79e710dda94e6c4d91a157317977f5b2388202bb63b08fcc325ea93f0453c.png)\n\nA gardener wants to create a flowerbed in the shape of a rectangle topped by a semicircle, as shown in the diagram. He needs the flowerbed to have an area of $\\frac{25}{9}\\,{cm}^{2}.$ What is the radius $r$ of the flowerbed with the smallest possible perimeter?" :question/worked-solution "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $x$ the side of the rectangle.\nThen, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=\\pi r+2x+2r.\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $\\frac{25}{9}$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n\\frac{25}{9}=2rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $x$ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{25}{9} & =2rx+\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2} \\\\ 2rx & =\\frac{25}{9}-\\frac{\\pi {r}^{2}}{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{25}{18r}-\\frac{\\pi r}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\pi r+2x+2r \\\\  & =\\pi r+2(\\frac{25}{18r}-\\frac{\\pi r}{4})+2r \\\\  & =\\pi r+\\frac{25}{9r}-\\frac{\\pi r}{2}+2r \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2)r+\\frac{25}{9r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2-\\frac{25}{9{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2-\\frac{25}{9{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 9{r}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{50}{9(\\pi +4)} \\\\ r & =\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{50}{9{r}^{3}}>0$, the stationary point $r=\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}$ is a minimum." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-3a81c85c-e7bb-5c20-a42f-21dc54ac708f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-3a81c85c-e7bb-5c20-a42f-21dc54ac708f" :answer-field/id #uuid "3a81c85c-e7bb-5c20-a42f-21dc54ac708f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-be8d6e20-b2d5-5f4a-9454-c6990f20a7de" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6e485939-81fc-5180-9df4-9c4801683555" "answer-f0c619ff-7a41-5aee-a283-e50b36fb5436" "answer-179f7f9c-7c2f-5ad0-991f-1bbdd00ec824" "answer-be8d6e20-b2d5-5f4a-9454-c6990f20a7de" "answer-84592676-b46c-5846-b30f-ce8c1c8efa34"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6e485939-81fc-5180-9df4-9c4801683555" :answer/id #uuid "6e485939-81fc-5180-9df4-9c4801683555" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{\\pi +4}}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f0c619ff-7a41-5aee-a283-e50b36fb5436" :answer/id #uuid "f0c619ff-7a41-5aee-a283-e50b36fb5436" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-179f7f9c-7c2f-5ad0-991f-1bbdd00ec824" :answer/id #uuid "179f7f9c-7c2f-5ad0-991f-1bbdd00ec824" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +2}}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be8d6e20-b2d5-5f4a-9454-c6990f20a7de" :answer/id #uuid "be8d6e20-b2d5-5f4a-9454-c6990f20a7de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{\\pi +4}}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-84592676-b46c-5846-b30f-ce8c1c8efa34" :answer/id #uuid "84592676-b46c-5846-b30f-ce8c1c8efa34" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}\\sqrt{\\frac{3}{\\pi +2}}\\,\\text{cm}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b59b20a8-8e7c-533f-bc4e-e028196934bf" :question/id #uuid "b59b20a8-8e7c-533f-bc4e-e028196934bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-315941" :question/problem "![](images/be/be50f6313cb20e06c3d850a3e81f267ac5e2cdc5c2a308280ba6367c08d66fec.png)\n\nMatt is planning a field whose surface is made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. The area of the field must be $9\\,{km}^{2}.$ What should be the radius $r$ if Matt wants a field with the smallest possible perimeter? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{km}$" :question/worked-solution "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the other side of the rectangle. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2x+\\pi r+\\pi r \\\\  & =2x+2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $9$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n9=2rx+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $2x$ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9 & =2rx+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2rx & =9-\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{9}{r}-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{9}{r}-\\pi r+2\\pi r \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{r}+\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(r) & =-\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi  & =0 \\\\ \\pi  & =\\frac{9}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{\\pi }} \\\\ r & =\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{18}{{r}^{3}}>0,$ the stationary point $r=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}$ is a minimum." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5de83ee2-eca3-5af9-9209-a72f1eca0796"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5de83ee2-eca3-5af9-9209-a72f1eca0796" :answer-field/id #uuid "5de83ee2-eca3-5af9-9209-a72f1eca0796" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1c220bd5-a7c3-540a-9148-f781412b5305" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1c220bd5-a7c3-540a-9148-f781412b5305"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c220bd5-a7c3-540a-9148-f781412b5305" :answer/id #uuid "1c220bd5-a7c3-540a-9148-f781412b5305" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{\\pi}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3990dbc6-49ea-5002-bf6b-028dcf68d1bf" :question/id #uuid "3990dbc6-49ea-5002-bf6b-028dcf68d1bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-345016" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0ef7a2645946099b10772fdc3fee46c582ad43debb0cb53041c46aa99c203ab1.png)\n\nSofia is designing a park that is made up of a rectangle and two semicircles, as shown in the diagram. The area of the park must be $16\\,{km}^{2}.$ What should be the radius $r$ if Sofia wants a park with the smallest possible perimeter? Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{km}$" :question/worked-solution "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector (the same for both semicircles) and let $x$ be the other side of the rectangle. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2x+\\pi r+\\pi r \\\\  & =2x+2\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $16\\text{ km},$ so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n16=2rx+\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $2x$ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =2rx+\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2rx & =16-\\pi {r}^{2} \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{16}{r}-\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =\\frac{16}{r}-\\pi r+2\\pi r \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{r}+\\pi r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(r) & =-\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}}+\\pi  & =0 \\\\ \\pi  & =\\frac{16}{{r}^{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{16}{\\pi } \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{16}{\\pi }} \\\\ r & =\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{32}{{r}^{3}}>0,$ the stationary point $r=\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi }}\\,\\text{km}$ is a minimum." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-73ecf20a-f4fe-5988-bf3c-7b7d82479ab8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-73ecf20a-f4fe-5988-bf3c-7b7d82479ab8" :answer-field/id #uuid "73ecf20a-f4fe-5988-bf3c-7b7d82479ab8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-1a859638-2f37-5243-a583-e0227a8f23fa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1a859638-2f37-5243-a583-e0227a8f23fa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a859638-2f37-5243-a583-e0227a8f23fa" :answer/id #uuid "1a859638-2f37-5243-a583-e0227a8f23fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{\\pi}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7c9b4d08-b984-5348-9fbd-0d702cd0c142" :question/id #uuid "7c9b4d08-b984-5348-9fbd-0d702cd0c142" :question/math-academy-id "q-344931" :question/problem "![](images/d2/d21626823e213a4c2d198f42e8e66d353364e722c1add41b35fcbc138ed8bff4.png)\n\nConsider all circular sectors $JKL$ with area $A=121$ square units. Determine the radius $r$ of the circular sector with the smallest possible perimeter. Do not round the answer.\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}\nunits" :question/worked-solution "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $121$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n121=\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta .\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}121 & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{242}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2r+r\\cdot \\frac{242}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =2r+\\frac{242}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=2-\\frac{242}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{242}{{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =242 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =121 \\\\ r & =11.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=11$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{484}{{r}^{3}}>0,$ the stationary point $r=11$ is a minimum.\n\nTherefore, the radius of the circular sector with the smallest perimeter is $r=11$ units." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d7dafff5-b3a8-518e-8b51-5bc2eaf0b1e0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d7dafff5-b3a8-518e-8b51-5bc2eaf0b1e0" :answer-field/id #uuid "d7dafff5-b3a8-518e-8b51-5bc2eaf0b1e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-7f9b529d-152c-55af-89ef-1953f5ea8685" :answer-field/choices ["answer-7f9b529d-152c-55af-89ef-1953f5ea8685"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7f9b529d-152c-55af-89ef-1953f5ea8685" :answer/id #uuid "7f9b529d-152c-55af-89ef-1953f5ea8685" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "11" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186102009 :tutorial/content "We can use differentiation to solve problems involving sectors of circles, such as finding the maximum area of the sector of a circle given a fixed perimeter. Let's remind ourselves of the general strategy for optimization problems.\n\n1\\. Draw a diagram of the situation, introducing variables where necessary.\n\n2\\. Write down the equation of the quantity that you're trying to maximize or minimize. This is called the **objective function**, and it might include more than one variable.\n\n3\\. Write down the constraint equation.\n\n4\\. Use the constraint equation to write the objective function in terms of a single variable only.\n\n5\\. Differentiate the objective function, set it equal to zero, and solve for the stationary points.\n\n6\\. Test each stationary point using the second derivative test. Or you can use the first derivative test if it's easier." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186101067 :activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186283994 :answer-field/choices 17592186283243] [:db/retract 17592186401489 :answer-field/choices 17592186401492] [:db/retract 17592186124878 :question/answer-fields 17592186121868] {:db/id 17592186124587 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-2ff3c43f-106f-5820-9db0-287d2d56d9d9"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124588 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c57b9d4a-351a-58d6-893f-d761a9a6ee5e"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186284488 :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our parabolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}=3y,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:(x-1)^{2}=3y\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is an upward-opening parabola with a vertex at $(1,0).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186284487 :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our hyperbolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{8}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+6)^{2}}{8}=1\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola centered at $(3,-6).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186283994 :answer-field/choices ["answer-b9fb8e6f-6307-55e5-aa75-c8d34607dc84"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9fb8e6f-6307-55e5-aa75-c8d34607dc84" :answer/id #uuid "b9fb8e6f-6307-55e5-aa75-c8d34607dc84" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:x\\ge 3\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186381313 :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our parabolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=-5(y+1),\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:{x}^{2}=-5(y+1)\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is a downward-opening parabola with a vertex at $(0,-1).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186401488 :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our hyperbolic cylinder is\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{4}=1\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is a hyperbola centered at $(0,-1).$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186401489 :answer-field/choices ["answer-e5818807-d65a-5f49-aebe-5e5a28895c19"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5818807-d65a-5f49-aebe-5e5a28895c19" :answer/id #uuid "e5818807-d65a-5f49-aebe-5e5a28895c19" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\text{ }x-y>12\\}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2ff3c43f-106f-5820-9db0-287d2d56d9d9" :question/id #uuid "2ff3c43f-106f-5820-9db0-287d2d56d9d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-164423" :question/problem "What is the domain in the $xy$-plane of the elliptic cylinder $(x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1?$" :question/worked-solution "The full expression describing all points on our elliptic cylinder is\n\n$$\n(x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1,\\,z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in R$ can vary arbitrarily. \n\nThe domain of any cylinder in the $xy$-plane is its base curve. Hence, in this case, the domain is\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:(x+3)^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{25}=1\\}\n$$\n\nwhich can be written as\n\n$$\n\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}:\\frac{(x+3)^{2}}{{1}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1\\}.\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse centered at $(-3,0)$ with semi-axes of lengths $1$ and $5.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-61868b69-fdbd-5aff-bb91-accdf71cfa90"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-61868b69-fdbd-5aff-bb91-accdf71cfa90" :answer-field/id #uuid "61868b69-fdbd-5aff-bb91-accdf71cfa90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d208bca8-3704-5c6a-88be-21b04308174b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d208bca8-3704-5c6a-88be-21b04308174b" "answer-f3b0ef1b-8d1b-5903-bf0c-073528025c0e" "answer-1af26990-37d6-5c3a-9040-4ef6f6b239f1" "answer-5022fcc6-150a-5502-aae7-0c36cf0e8742" "answer-c127f29f-052a-5794-b5b2-3be574c6f2dd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d208bca8-3704-5c6a-88be-21b04308174b" :answer/id #uuid "d208bca8-3704-5c6a-88be-21b04308174b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(-3,0)$ with semi-axes of lengths $1$ and $5.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3b0ef1b-8d1b-5903-bf0c-073528025c0e" :answer/id #uuid "f3b0ef1b-8d1b-5903-bf0c-073528025c0e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(-3,0)$ with semi-axes of lengths $1$ and $\\sqrt{5}.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1af26990-37d6-5c3a-9040-4ef6f6b239f1" :answer/id #uuid "1af26990-37d6-5c3a-9040-4ef6f6b239f1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An ellipse centered at $(3,0)$ with semi-axes of lengths $1$ and $25.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5022fcc6-150a-5502-aae7-0c36cf0e8742" :answer/id #uuid "5022fcc6-150a-5502-aae7-0c36cf0e8742" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The interior and boundary of an ellipse centered at $(-3,0)$ with semi-axes of lengths $1$ and $5.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c127f29f-052a-5794-b5b2-3be574c6f2dd" :answer/id #uuid "c127f29f-052a-5794-b5b2-3be574c6f2dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The interior and boundary of an ellipse centered at $(3,0)$ with semi-axes of lengths $1$ and $25.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c57b9d4a-351a-58d6-893f-d761a9a6ee5e" :question/id #uuid "c57b9d4a-351a-58d6-893f-d761a9a6ee5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-164404" :question/problem "What is the trace in the $xy$-plane of the parabolic cylinder $(y-4)^{2}=z+1?$" :question/worked-solution "To find the trace in the $xy$-plane, we substitute $z=0$ into the equation of the cylinder, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =0+1 \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =1 \\\\ y-4 & =\\pm 1 \\\\ y & =4\\pm 1 \\\\ y & =3,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of two lines $y=3$ and $y=5$ in the $xy$-plane." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9738db51-6bb6-5a1e-9a88-9854819dcf8d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9738db51-6bb6-5a1e-9a88-9854819dcf8d" :answer-field/id #uuid "9738db51-6bb6-5a1e-9a88-9854819dcf8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-6ea2ecb9-d2a8-5867-bf21-c375717be587" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a7da925a-f55d-52ac-9d41-103de4c6c2d3" "answer-65bb6a4d-bacc-573d-8ac9-de1124e3b120" "answer-6ea2ecb9-d2a8-5867-bf21-c375717be587" "answer-d573fd0d-d62b-50c0-9361-ac2dddd6713c" "answer-4e5ab69b-2ab8-5b94-86d2-1d106a5722a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a7da925a-f55d-52ac-9d41-103de4c6c2d3" :answer/id #uuid "a7da925a-f55d-52ac-9d41-103de4c6c2d3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The line $y=4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65bb6a4d-bacc-573d-8ac9-de1124e3b120" :answer/id #uuid "65bb6a4d-bacc-573d-8ac9-de1124e3b120" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The line $y=5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6ea2ecb9-d2a8-5867-bf21-c375717be587" :answer/id #uuid "6ea2ecb9-d2a8-5867-bf21-c375717be587" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The lines $y=3$ and $y=5$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d573fd0d-d62b-50c0-9361-ac2dddd6713c" :answer/id #uuid "d573fd0d-d62b-50c0-9361-ac2dddd6713c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The parabola $(y-4)^{2}=x+1$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e5ab69b-2ab8-5b94-86d2-1d106a5722a0" :answer/id #uuid "4e5ab69b-2ab8-5b94-86d2-1d106a5722a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The parabola ${y}^{2}=x+4$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125830 :tutorial/content "A **cylinder** is a surface made by the set of lines that pass through a plane curve $C$ in the $xy$-plane and are perpendicular to this plane. The curve $C$ is called the **base curve**.\n\nThe familiar two-dimensional conic sections (circles, ellipses, parabolas, and hyperbolas) can be used to describe cylinders. In this lesson, we'll explore these shapes.\n\nAn **elliptic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is an ellipse. If the ellipse lies in the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of our elliptic cylinder is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are the semi-axes of the ellipse $C.$ In particular, if $a=b$, we get a **circular cylinder.**\n\n**Watch out!** This may look like the equation of a regular ellipse. However, since we're now describing a surface in three-dimensional space, we assume that the $z$-coordinate is arbitrary. Thus, if we want $z$ to feature in our equations explicitly, we would write\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1, \\qquad z=k\n$$\n\nwhere $k\\in \\mathbb R.$ \n\nFor example, the graph of the elliptic cylinder\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + y^2 = 1\n$$\n\nis shown below. The center of its base ellipse $C$ is at the origin, and the **axis** of the elliptic cylinder (the line through the center of the cylinder) is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/50/504e609e134654d258378226d90f5faa8b9f6d12313a4fcac4a23d0bfe6bea52.png)\n\nLet's describe some properties of this elliptic cylinder:\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{4} + y^2 = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the base ellipse $C$ centered at the origin $(0,0,0)$ with semi-major and semi-minor axes of lengths $2$ and $1,$ respectively.\n\n![](images/d0/d0e4c0630b0da60c4324330211ab1bf8090ebec067d9ebc2ac2a03056c500bd6.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4 \\\\ x & =\\pm 2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to two lines in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/38/3813a5f43319056be03ddd8b3353ef3a8166b7495b32397c91066f40d0b33cd5.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{0}^{2}}{4}+{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ y & =\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to two lines in the $yz$-plane.\n\n![](images/a0/a0d928060ad18d3ba677f5dd184777b833d3abf03226c04c21292e455bcd0518.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125831 :tutorial/content "A **parabolic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is a parabola.\n\nIf the parabola $C$ opens upward or downward when viewed from the $xy$-plane and its vertex is at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the parabolic cylinder is given by\n\n$$\n(x-x_0)^2 = 4p(y-y_0),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nFor example, the graph of the parabolic cylinder\n\n$$\nx^2 = y\n$$\n\nis shown below. Its base curve is an upward-opening parabola in the $xy$-plane with its vertex at the origin. The **axis** of the cylinder is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/03/03ab0a28802900d8076fdd5f555bdad6582c449441c46f1de74260013cc55119.png)\n\nThe trace of this parabolic cylinder can be either a parabola or a set of lines.\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 = y,\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to the base parabola $C$ with its vertex at the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/98/98124890d921c73d0cda54428ac803221d5f393384e14db2909b0138fda0fb93.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to a line in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/f8/f87dbd180911db28a515dfe11da99d5d9d06da74e7ef117ea0a6df88018e95a2.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{0}^{2} & =y \\\\ y & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to a line in the $yz$-plane.\n\n![](images/f8/f87dbd180911db28a515dfe11da99d5d9d06da74e7ef117ea0a6df88018e95a2.png)\n\nOn the other hand, if the base parabola $C$ is left-opening or right-opening when viewed from the $xy$-plane and its vertex is at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the parabolic cylinder is given by\n\n$$\n(y-y_0)^2 = 4p(x-x_0),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nFor example, the graph of the parabolic cylinder\n\n$$\ny^2 = x\n$$\n\nis shown below. Its base curve is a right-opening parabola in the $xy$-plane with its vertex at the origin. The cylinder's axis is the $z$-axis.\n\n  \n\n![](images/a8/a87267dbd77b1561c047c6228ba0c70b08e5a48228d7e0b6a161463045d5e22b.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125834 :tutorial/content "A **hyperbolic cylinder** is a cylinder whose base curve $C$ is a hyperbola.\n\nIf the hyperbola $C$ is horizontal in the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the hyperbolic cylinder is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\n For example, the graph of the hyperbola cylinder\n\n$$\nx^2 - y^2 = 1\n$$\n\nis shown below. Its base curve is a horizontal hyperbola in the $xy$-plane centered at the origin. The **axis** of the cylinder is the $z$-axis.\n\n![](images/e3/e3c921cc7d6a1edbb182ecf158d7b84eb0d203fd20924d07cf57ca8f8ffdc107.png)\n\nThe trace of this surface can be either a hyperbola, a set of lines, or the empty set.\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xy$-plane (when $z=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\nx^2 - y^2 = 1,\n$$\n\nwhich is the base hyperbola $C$ centered at the origin $(0,0,0).$\n\n![](images/19/19e02f27f625dd75d12333de3b82a7a012e4f6370c729eef5cd38a633c2c8689.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $xz$-plane (when $y=0$) is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-{0}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ x & =\\pm 1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich corresponds to a pair of lines in the $xz$-plane.\n\n![](images/56/5679b53433493d8b6e92d5aa5273be30bbf67a9db51993a925423d8514571f05.png)\n\n- The trace of this surface in the $yz$-plane (when $x=0$) is the empty set.\n\nOn the other hand, if the base hyperbola $C$ is vertical when viewed from the $xy$-plane and is centered at $(x_0,y_0,0)$, then the equation of the hyperbolic cylinder is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{(y-y_0)^2}{b^2} - \\dfrac{(x-x_0)^2}{a^2} = 1,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nFor example, the graph of the hyperbola cylinder\n\n$$\ny^2 - x^2 = 1\n$$\n\nis shown below. Its base curve is a vertical hyperbola in the $xy$-plane centered at the origin, and the cylinder's axis is the $z$-axis.\n\n  \n\n![](images/d6/d690bca9515389bb403d0f205861af3294bc3a961df6691a5adb86de01421cff.png)\n\nNote that for all of the cylinders discussed so far, the *domain* of each cylinder in the $xy$-plane is simply the base curve $C.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124698 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176022 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-00868e65-0caa-53d3-bac1-8c3f5e5b6143" "question-981ba9c5-75b9-5f37-b62a-2b2258f28bdb" "question-c8a37d3e-d129-5ae7-8ba8-cbd709bbd8bf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-00868e65-0caa-53d3-bac1-8c3f5e5b6143" :question/id #uuid "00868e65-0caa-53d3-bac1-8c3f5e5b6143" :question/math-academy-id "q-118712" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(x+1)+1}{x+1})}^{2x+2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{2(x+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $x+1=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{x+2}{x+1})}^{2x+2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{x+1})}^{2(x+1)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9e72dc91-9bb2-5a52-9ea8-c8a3392b078f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9e72dc91-9bb2-5a52-9ea8-c8a3392b078f" :answer-field/id #uuid "9e72dc91-9bb2-5a52-9ea8-c8a3392b078f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3f0635c7-95c2-5ca0-b103-4151020dda21" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fdebe404-8aed-5769-8c60-015f60d99e5e" "answer-7affb6d5-dcf4-5d5f-997f-81bf1706c88d" "answer-3cc1ba37-52fc-50dc-b984-9dc568bfe31c" "answer-fef041c0-b3ba-511a-a1db-5120dc4f98a7" "answer-3f0635c7-95c2-5ca0-b103-4151020dda21"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fdebe404-8aed-5769-8c60-015f60d99e5e" :answer/id #uuid "fdebe404-8aed-5769-8c60-015f60d99e5e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7affb6d5-dcf4-5d5f-997f-81bf1706c88d" :answer/id #uuid "7affb6d5-dcf4-5d5f-997f-81bf1706c88d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3cc1ba37-52fc-50dc-b984-9dc568bfe31c" :answer/id #uuid "3cc1ba37-52fc-50dc-b984-9dc568bfe31c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fef041c0-b3ba-511a-a1db-5120dc4f98a7" :answer/id #uuid "fef041c0-b3ba-511a-a1db-5120dc4f98a7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{e}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f0635c7-95c2-5ca0-b103-4151020dda21" :answer/id #uuid "3f0635c7-95c2-5ca0-b103-4151020dda21" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-981ba9c5-75b9-5f37-b62a-2b2258f28bdb" :question/id #uuid "981ba9c5-75b9-5f37-b62a-2b2258f28bdb" :question/math-academy-id "q-118714" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3x})}^{2(3x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $3x=n.$ Therefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{3x+1}{3x})}^{6x} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3x})}^{2(3x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2n} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{2} \\\\  & ={e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ac530e0e-d3d1-5a9a-9415-9ac86edc2ff1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ac530e0e-d3d1-5a9a-9415-9ac86edc2ff1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ac530e0e-d3d1-5a9a-9415-9ac86edc2ff1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-24156e2e-8841-555a-a19b-1a625b98dde5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-35e61fc0-40f6-5aef-922f-50f62512b14d" "answer-24156e2e-8841-555a-a19b-1a625b98dde5" "answer-141e356a-c0b9-53fb-a3fa-1c304d6a2600" "answer-8cfcba74-2e37-531b-b078-1a79a99a831c" "answer-5e34251a-f4f3-549b-a7a0-aeaadf3cbdfd"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-35e61fc0-40f6-5aef-922f-50f62512b14d" :answer/id #uuid "35e61fc0-40f6-5aef-922f-50f62512b14d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-24156e2e-8841-555a-a19b-1a625b98dde5" :answer/id #uuid "24156e2e-8841-555a-a19b-1a625b98dde5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-141e356a-c0b9-53fb-a3fa-1c304d6a2600" :answer/id #uuid "141e356a-c0b9-53fb-a3fa-1c304d6a2600" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cfcba74-2e37-531b-b078-1a79a99a831c" :answer/id #uuid "8cfcba74-2e37-531b-b078-1a79a99a831c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e34251a-f4f3-549b-a7a0-aeaadf3cbdfd" :answer/id #uuid "5e34251a-f4f3-549b-a7a0-aeaadf3cbdfd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c8a37d3e-d129-5ae7-8ba8-cbd709bbd8bf" :question/id #uuid "c8a37d3e-d129-5ae7-8ba8-cbd709bbd8bf" :question/math-academy-id "q-118721" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given limit as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{(2x-2)+1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})}^{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $2(x-1)=n$ Then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x-1) & =n \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{n}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{2x-1}{2x-2})}^{x-1} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{2(x-1)})}^{x-1} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/2} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/2} \\\\  & ={e}^{1/2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c7bcd67e-475b-5366-91b7-b32feba0f989"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c7bcd67e-475b-5366-91b7-b32feba0f989" :answer-field/id #uuid "c7bcd67e-475b-5366-91b7-b32feba0f989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-31e0be61-4604-5a58-b042-bc92fc457d48" :answer-field/choices ["answer-edae2223-31c8-57b8-91a2-c2b71c6e6090" "answer-f7b08025-f86f-5c45-b65e-e33d0d2c521c" "answer-19cd2332-d9e4-5f07-a6f6-b1f461806d71" "answer-31e0be61-4604-5a58-b042-bc92fc457d48" "answer-f7b94c54-bdfd-5cd4-a505-1c37eeea500c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edae2223-31c8-57b8-91a2-c2b71c6e6090" :answer/id #uuid "edae2223-31c8-57b8-91a2-c2b71c6e6090" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7b08025-f86f-5c45-b65e-e33d0d2c521c" :answer/id #uuid "f7b08025-f86f-5c45-b65e-e33d0d2c521c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{e}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-19cd2332-d9e4-5f07-a6f6-b1f461806d71" :answer/id #uuid "19cd2332-d9e4-5f07-a6f6-b1f461806d71" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31e0be61-4604-5a58-b042-bc92fc457d48" :answer/id #uuid "31e0be61-4604-5a58-b042-bc92fc457d48" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f7b94c54-bdfd-5cd4-a505-1c37eeea500c" :answer/id #uuid "f7b94c54-bdfd-5cd4-a505-1c37eeea500c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186176019 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-91b96bb8-d0ec-5384-8420-38fd513a4a78" "question-cff12c80-2e8b-52fc-a40b-42f5ea296e0f"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-91b96bb8-d0ec-5384-8420-38fd513a4a78" :question/id #uuid "91b96bb8-d0ec-5384-8420-38fd513a4a78" :question/math-academy-id "q-127543" :question/problem "Evaluate ${lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3t-6})}^{t-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $t\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3t-6})}^{t-2}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nIn the given limit, we let $3t-6=n.$ Thus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3t-6 & =n \\\\ 3(t-2) & =n \\\\ t-2 & =\\frac{n}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $t\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{3t-6})}^{t-2} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n/3} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{1/3} \\\\  & ={e}^{1/3} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{e}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b4d49d40-4bea-500d-bdf8-019158dcd1ee"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b4d49d40-4bea-500d-bdf8-019158dcd1ee" :answer-field/id #uuid "b4d49d40-4bea-500d-bdf8-019158dcd1ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-142517b8-196c-572d-bb30-b593804d312b" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d1fa933c-70d4-5eac-8738-8875677c238c" "answer-eab3a712-8119-5c80-9935-77d99f9b65c0" "answer-142517b8-196c-572d-bb30-b593804d312b" "answer-b79e0202-007e-5255-baf6-8ab1013d2835" "answer-2e13745f-0660-51aa-b3c9-308fbcc11a7e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d1fa933c-70d4-5eac-8738-8875677c238c" :answer/id #uuid "d1fa933c-70d4-5eac-8738-8875677c238c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{e}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eab3a712-8119-5c80-9935-77d99f9b65c0" :answer/id #uuid "eab3a712-8119-5c80-9935-77d99f9b65c0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{e}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-142517b8-196c-572d-bb30-b593804d312b" :answer/id #uuid "142517b8-196c-572d-bb30-b593804d312b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b79e0202-007e-5255-baf6-8ab1013d2835" :answer/id #uuid "b79e0202-007e-5255-baf6-8ab1013d2835" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt[3]{{e}^{2}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2e13745f-0660-51aa-b3c9-308fbcc11a7e" :answer/id #uuid "2e13745f-0660-51aa-b3c9-308fbcc11a7e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{{e}^{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cff12c80-2e8b-52fc-a40b-42f5ea296e0f" :question/id #uuid "cff12c80-2e8b-52fc-a40b-42f5ea296e0f" :question/math-academy-id "q-338566" :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{3}{x})}^{2x}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to \\infty ,$ \n\n- the expression inside the parentheses approaches $1,$ while\n\n- the exponent approaches $\\infty .$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{3}{x})}^{2x}={1}^{\\infty },\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}=e\n$$\n\nIn the given limit, we let $\\frac{3}{x}=\\frac{1}{n}.$ Thus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n} \\\\ x & =3n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $n\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we rewrite the given limit using the algebra of limits, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{3}{x})}^{2x} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{2(3n)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{[{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{6} \\\\  & ={[{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n}]}^{6} \\\\  & ={e}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eb677ded-facb-5ee5-9acc-a1da685a4a6b"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eb677ded-facb-5ee5-9acc-a1da685a4a6b" :answer-field/id #uuid "eb677ded-facb-5ee5-9acc-a1da685a4a6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-9c1642b7-884e-5096-ad66-1a3b13f3c305" :answer-field/choices ["answer-9c1642b7-884e-5096-ad66-1a3b13f3c305"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c1642b7-884e-5096-ad66-1a3b13f3c305" :answer/id #uuid "9c1642b7-884e-5096-ad66-1a3b13f3c305" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e^{6}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-f2d15eff-4bf3-528d-a472-58ed556f58f2" :activity/id #uuid "f2d15eff-4bf3-528d-a472-58ed556f58f2" :activity/title "Further Limits Involving the Exponential Function" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049325 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-6a3949a3-5808-5f9d-a44a-dabfca5b2e15" "step-3145e340-cda7-5adf-9900-0d495fc4e789" "step-b6ed0ab8-eebb-5a58-85d6-828a7ea195c3" "step-c4026a05-5a37-59aa-a6fd-1b0c2c9aa547" "step-99de8130-883e-53ab-99b6-1a108d294a59"] :activity/first-step "step-6a3949a3-5808-5f9d-a44a-dabfca5b2e15" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-6a3949a3-5808-5f9d-a44a-dabfca5b2e15" :step/id #uuid "6a3949a3-5808-5f9d-a44a-dabfca5b2e15" :step/content "question-00868e65-0caa-53d3-bac1-8c3f5e5b6143" :step/next "step-3145e340-cda7-5adf-9900-0d495fc4e789" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3145e340-cda7-5adf-9900-0d495fc4e789" :step/id #uuid "3145e340-cda7-5adf-9900-0d495fc4e789" :step/content "question-981ba9c5-75b9-5f37-b62a-2b2258f28bdb" :step/next "step-b6ed0ab8-eebb-5a58-85d6-828a7ea195c3" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-b6ed0ab8-eebb-5a58-85d6-828a7ea195c3" :step/id #uuid "b6ed0ab8-eebb-5a58-85d6-828a7ea195c3" :step/content "question-c8a37d3e-d129-5ae7-8ba8-cbd709bbd8bf" :step/next "step-c4026a05-5a37-59aa-a6fd-1b0c2c9aa547" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c4026a05-5a37-59aa-a6fd-1b0c2c9aa547" :step/id #uuid "c4026a05-5a37-59aa-a6fd-1b0c2c9aa547" :step/content "question-91b96bb8-d0ec-5384-8420-38fd513a4a78" :step/next "step-99de8130-883e-53ab-99b6-1a108d294a59" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-99de8130-883e-53ab-99b6-1a108d294a59" :step/id #uuid "99de8130-883e-53ab-99b6-1a108d294a59" :step/content "question-cff12c80-2e8b-52fc-a40b-42f5ea296e0f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186058494 :answer-field/choices 17592186058496] [:db/retract 17592186058494 :answer-field/choices 17592186058497] [:db/retract 17592186058494 :answer-field/choices 17592186058498] [:db/retract 17592186058494 :answer-field/choices 17592186058499] [:db/retract 17592186058494 :answer-field/choices 17592186058495] {:db/id 17592186059870 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a3a40784-83f5-5659-8e12-9e2f515c5795" "question-e2edca84-13da-5da7-b4b8-ed4b37f13789"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059461 :question/problem "A radioactive substance decays exponentially such that its mass reduces by  $\\dfrac 1 3$ every year. If the mass of the substance was initially  $4\\,\\textrm{kg},$  what is the mass of the substance after $6$ years?" :question/worked-solution "If the mass of the substance reduces by $\\dfrac 1 3$ every year, then $\\dfrac 2 3$ of its mass remains every year.\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, the mass is $4\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 2 3\\right)\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\n\n- After $\\color{blue}2$ years, the mass is $4\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 2 3\\right)^{\\color{blue}2}\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\n\n- After $\\color{blue}3$ years, the mass is $4\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 2 3\\right)^{\\color{blue}3}\\,\\textrm{kg}.$\n\n- $\\ldots$\n\nRepeating this pattern, we see that the mass after $\\color{blue}6$ years is $4\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 2 3\\right)^{\\color{blue}6}\\,\\textrm{kg}.$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059869 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-38f4d55c-c716-54e2-ab1e-afc12e3f81f0"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059460 :question/worked-solution "The number $12\\%$ written as a decimal is $0.12.$ Therefore, a decrease of $12\\%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of\n\n$$\n1-0.12 = 0.88.\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, the annual profit was $40\\,000\\cdot (0.88)$ dollars.\n\n- After $\\color{blue}2$ years, the annual profit was $40\\,000\\cdot (0.88)^{\\color{blue}2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $\\color{blue}3$ years, the annual profit was $40\\,000\\cdot (0.88)^{\\color{blue}3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $\\color{blue}4$ years, the annual profit $40\\,000\\cdot (0.88)^{\\color{blue}4}$ dollars.\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the last expression:\n\n$$\n\\[math]40\\,000\\cdot (0.88)^{4} \\approx \\[/math]23\\,988\n$$\n\nTherefore, Carlton's profit reduced to $\\[math]23\\,988$ after $4$ years." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a3a40784-83f5-5659-8e12-9e2f515c5795" :question/id #uuid "a3a40784-83f5-5659-8e12-9e2f515c5795" :question/math-academy-id "q-45481" :question/problem "The price of a new bicycle is $$625.$ If the bicycle loses $\\frac{1}{5}$ of its value every year, what will be its value after $4$ years?" :question/worked-solution "Since the value of the bicycle reduces by $\\frac{1}{5}$ per year, $\\frac{4}{5}$ of its value remains every year. \n\nLet's calculate the value of the bicycle after every year:\n\n- After $1$ year, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot (\\frac{4}{5})$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ years, the bicycle will be worth $625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{4}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the last expression, we get\n\n$$\n625\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{5})}^{4}=256.\n$$\n\nSo after $4$ years, the bicycle will be worth $$256.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a6663f5d-bdaf-51e5-a9ac-ff63ad1ed5e7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a6663f5d-bdaf-51e5-a9ac-ff63ad1ed5e7" :answer-field/id #uuid "a6663f5d-bdaf-51e5-a9ac-ff63ad1ed5e7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c99df81f-1e1f-537a-baa9-d9d490cfcc66" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ff6283c6-2d8e-5486-863d-299a65917ed1" "answer-c63d24bd-4157-511a-865f-5dd14362508a" "answer-c99df81f-1e1f-537a-baa9-d9d490cfcc66" "answer-d47f863c-e4dd-5cb8-9cc2-dd2c380f778e" "answer-a4e028ec-1409-5643-a03a-1db8045367c7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ff6283c6-2d8e-5486-863d-299a65917ed1" :answer/id #uuid "ff6283c6-2d8e-5486-863d-299a65917ed1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$1\\,024$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c63d24bd-4157-511a-865f-5dd14362508a" :answer/id #uuid "c63d24bd-4157-511a-865f-5dd14362508a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$1\\,280$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c99df81f-1e1f-537a-baa9-d9d490cfcc66" :answer/id #uuid "c99df81f-1e1f-537a-baa9-d9d490cfcc66" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$256$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d47f863c-e4dd-5cb8-9cc2-dd2c380f778e" :answer/id #uuid "d47f863c-e4dd-5cb8-9cc2-dd2c380f778e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$320$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a4e028ec-1409-5643-a03a-1db8045367c7" :answer/id #uuid "a4e028ec-1409-5643-a03a-1db8045367c7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$640$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e2edca84-13da-5da7-b4b8-ed4b37f13789" :question/id #uuid "e2edca84-13da-5da7-b4b8-ed4b37f13789" :question/math-academy-id "q-26244" :question/problem "A pest controller was responsible for humanely reducing the number of mice within a hotel. If there were $720$ mice initially and $\\frac{5}{6}$ of mice were removed from the hotel every day, how many mice remained in the hotel after two days?" :question/worked-solution "Since the number of mice reduced by $\\frac{5}{6}$ per day, then only $\\frac{1}{6}$ of the mice from the previous day remained.\n\nSo we have:\n\n- At the start, there were $720$ mice.\n\n- After $1$ day, there were $720\\cdot (\\frac{1}{6})$ mice.\n\n- After $2$ days, there were $720\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}$ mice.\n\nEvaluating the last expression, we get\n\n$$\n720\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{6})}^{2}=20.\n$$\n\nTherefore, after $2$ days only $20$ mice remained." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-51810a08-334f-5c26-a631-f9e234750786"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-51810a08-334f-5c26-a631-f9e234750786" :answer-field/id #uuid "51810a08-334f-5c26-a631-f9e234750786" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4a32cd4b-193c-5d68-8944-db95ecc3226a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-97e76a18-2641-51b0-a183-4e17294d8733" "answer-1a77c3bd-e621-5fd7-b6c7-0706a0f09516" "answer-4a32cd4b-193c-5d68-8944-db95ecc3226a" "answer-6a031128-7cbf-5a5c-a079-c9a2ed669acd" "answer-13d5732d-43c0-52d1-8fcd-5b4c29c890e7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-97e76a18-2641-51b0-a183-4e17294d8733" :answer/id #uuid "97e76a18-2641-51b0-a183-4e17294d8733" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "120" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1a77c3bd-e621-5fd7-b6c7-0706a0f09516" :answer/id #uuid "1a77c3bd-e621-5fd7-b6c7-0706a0f09516" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "14" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4a32cd4b-193c-5d68-8944-db95ecc3226a" :answer/id #uuid "4a32cd4b-193c-5d68-8944-db95ecc3226a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6a031128-7cbf-5a5c-a079-c9a2ed669acd" :answer/id #uuid "6a031128-7cbf-5a5c-a079-c9a2ed669acd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "75" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-13d5732d-43c0-52d1-8fcd-5b4c29c890e7" :answer/id #uuid "13d5732d-43c0-52d1-8fcd-5b4c29c890e7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060312 :question/problem "A car was purchased for $$7\\,500$ and its value depreciated (decreased) by $15%$ per year. To the nearest dollar, what was the value of the car after $3$ years?" :question/worked-solution "The number $15%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.15.$ Therefore, a decrease of $15%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.15=0.85\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, the car's value was $7\\,500\\cdot (0.85)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, the car's value was $7\\,500\\cdot (0.85)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, the car's value was $7\\,500\\cdot (0.85)^{3}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the last expression gives\n\n$$\n7\\,500\\cdot (0.85)^{3}≈4\\,606.\n$$\n\nSo the car was worth $$4\\,606$ (to the nearest dollar) after $3$ years." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186058494 :answer-field/correct "answer-1cfb6c8f-22a9-50b3-a22c-91a50b2c3b34" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c4dc14c8-0985-5ed0-8e0a-76614989ad29" "answer-1cfb6c8f-22a9-50b3-a22c-91a50b2c3b34" "answer-3724d7ee-a391-55b1-b250-d93c5bb5f7f3" "answer-edac4472-e23d-5e0d-afbb-78d7587dfad4" "answer-0d884f04-bdfd-5f62-87a4-cc746e783256"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4dc14c8-0985-5ed0-8e0a-76614989ad29" :answer/id #uuid "c4dc14c8-0985-5ed0-8e0a-76614989ad29" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$4\\,224$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1cfb6c8f-22a9-50b3-a22c-91a50b2c3b34" :answer/id #uuid "1cfb6c8f-22a9-50b3-a22c-91a50b2c3b34" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$4\\,606$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3724d7ee-a391-55b1-b250-d93c5bb5f7f3" :answer/id #uuid "3724d7ee-a391-55b1-b250-d93c5bb5f7f3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$4\\,902$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-edac4472-e23d-5e0d-afbb-78d7587dfad4" :answer/id #uuid "edac4472-e23d-5e0d-afbb-78d7587dfad4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$5\\,067$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0d884f04-bdfd-5f62-87a4-cc746e783256" :answer/id #uuid "0d884f04-bdfd-5f62-87a4-cc746e783256" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$5\\,301$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-38f4d55c-c716-54e2-ab1e-afc12e3f81f0" :question/id #uuid "38f4d55c-c716-54e2-ab1e-afc12e3f81f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-63874" :question/problem "Olivia bought a home for $$150\\,000.$ The value of the home declined by $16%$ annually. To the nearest dollar, what was the value of the home after $4$ years?" :question/worked-solution "The number $16%$ (expressed as a decimal) is equal to $0.16.$ Therefore, a decrease of $16%$ corresponds to multiplication by a factor of:\n\n$$\n1-0.16=0.84\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- After $1$ year, value of the home was $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)$ dollars.\n\n- After $2$ years, value of the home was $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{2}$ dollars.\n\n- After $3$ years, value of the home was  $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{3}$ dollars.\n\n- After $4$ years, value of the home was $150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{4}$ dollars.\n\nEvaluating the last expression gives:\n\n$$\n150\\,000\\cdot (0.84)^{4}≈74\\,681\n$$\n\nSo, the home was worth $$74\\,681$ (to the nearest dollar) after $4$ years." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-98fc00d4-eaa0-5074-a7a2-52d9b8beb28f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-98fc00d4-eaa0-5074-a7a2-52d9b8beb28f" :answer-field/id #uuid "98fc00d4-eaa0-5074-a7a2-52d9b8beb28f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e2b5a82a-32f7-56ee-bf17-a5cd9187194a" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e2b5a82a-32f7-56ee-bf17-a5cd9187194a" "answer-bb85eec1-a8cc-5386-a093-8ff25c843a0b" "answer-45896a3d-55b3-509c-9a87-a9666e1661fa" "answer-7d406a7f-05b4-5ddd-b9da-ca2eb0b547f9" "answer-323b7c73-1649-5faf-b101-6a4ee721f31a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2b5a82a-32f7-56ee-bf17-a5cd9187194a" :answer/id #uuid "e2b5a82a-32f7-56ee-bf17-a5cd9187194a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$74\\,681$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bb85eec1-a8cc-5386-a093-8ff25c843a0b" :answer/id #uuid "bb85eec1-a8cc-5386-a093-8ff25c843a0b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$76\\,234$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-45896a3d-55b3-509c-9a87-a9666e1661fa" :answer/id #uuid "45896a3d-55b3-509c-9a87-a9666e1661fa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$77\\,501$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7d406a7f-05b4-5ddd-b9da-ca2eb0b547f9" :answer/id #uuid "7d406a7f-05b4-5ddd-b9da-ca2eb0b547f9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$79\\,402$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-323b7c73-1649-5faf-b101-6a4ee721f31a" :answer/id #uuid "323b7c73-1649-5faf-b101-6a4ee721f31a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$$84\\,346$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186060871 :tutorial/content "A situation involves **exponential decay** if some initial amount repeatedly shrinks due to being multiplied by some constant rate.\n\nFor example, suppose that a certain battery had a voltage of $4\\,\\textrm{V}$ when it was healthy. Following a sharp impact, the battery's voltage decreased by $\\dfrac12$ every day since the day of the impact. What was the voltage of the battery $3$ days after it was damaged?\n\nStarting with a voltage of $4\\,\\textrm{V},$ we know that this number will halve every day. So after $1$ day, the voltage (measured in volts) was\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}{4\\cdot \\dfrac 1 2}}.\n$$\n\nOn the second day, this number halved again. So the voltage after $2$ days was\n\n$$\n\\left({\\color{blue}{4\\cdot \\dfrac 1 2}}\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac 1 2 = {\\color{red}{4\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^2}}.\n$$\n\nAfter one more day, the number above halves once again. So the voltage after $3$ days was\n\n$$\n{\\color{red}{4\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^2}}\\cdot \\dfrac 1 2 = {\\color{black}{4\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^3}}.\n$$\n\nIf we evaluate the above expression, we get:\n\n$$\n4\\cdot \\left(\\dfrac 1 2\\right)^3 = 4\\cdot \\dfrac 1 8 =  \\dfrac 1 2\n$$\n\nSo after $3$ days, the voltage reduced to just $\\dfrac 1 2\\,\\textrm{V}.$\n\nThis is an example of exponential decay. The voltage decreases by $\\dfrac 1 2$ over a time interval of $1$ day, every day. The voltage decays very rapidly, as we can see from the graph below.\n\n![](images/ee/ee97a0d5495dd28c8e0af1168fa4dc0455f1985ca8ab8f9a8e8dbf3e1a84ae99.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186059879 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186231573 :question/answer-fields 17592186230307] [:db/retract 17592186230317 :answer-field/choices 17592186230320] [:db/retract 17592186230317 :answer-field/choices 17592186230321] [:db/retract 17592186230317 :answer-field/choices 17592186230318] [:db/retract 17592186230317 :answer-field/choices 17592186230319] {:db/id 17592186231406 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-a25da531-efbb-5c52-a54e-e70d588e0c03"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231575 :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+3y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-3,3]$, where $f$ is $6$-periodic and even. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Because $f$ is $6$-periodic on $[-3,3]$, we have $L=3$, so Fourier series on this interval use the basis\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\,\\text{and}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNext, since $f$ is even, its Fourier series contains only cosine terms. For a second-order equation with no ${y}^{′}$ (damping) term, the particular solution inherits this property and is also an even function.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate form for ${y}_{p}$ is a cosine series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)={\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186230317 :answer-field/correct "answer-846bcad3-1671-5fb8-b71c-e4babf98109d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-846bcad3-1671-5fb8-b71c-e4babf98109d" "answer-7899c111-a756-5f84-bb7b-8a3a2fc1f66f" "answer-08e980a7-73e5-5db5-b117-dad8065a2ce3" "answer-aff8259e-8a40-5b92-873a-8c1bbf4d08f2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-846bcad3-1671-5fb8-b71c-e4babf98109d" :answer/id #uuid "846bcad3-1671-5fb8-b71c-e4babf98109d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7899c111-a756-5f84-bb7b-8a3a2fc1f66f" :answer/id #uuid "7899c111-a756-5f84-bb7b-8a3a2fc1f66f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08e980a7-73e5-5db5-b117-dad8065a2ce3" :answer/id #uuid "08e980a7-73e5-5db5-b117-dad8065a2ce3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{6})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aff8259e-8a40-5b92-873a-8c1bbf4d08f2" :answer/id #uuid "aff8259e-8a40-5b92-873a-8c1bbf4d08f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186372854 :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x)\n$$\n\non $[-4,4]$, where $f$ is $8$-periodic and odd. Which Fourier-series form is appropriate for a particular solution ${y}_{p}?$\n\n$\\,$\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-a25da531-efbb-5c52-a54e-e70d588e0c03" :question/id #uuid "a25da531-efbb-5c52-a54e-e70d588e0c03" :question/math-academy-id "q-344320" :question/problem "Consider the boundary value problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+5y=f(x),\\,y(0)=0,\\,y(1)=6\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)$ is $4$-periodic and even. Select the missing part in the solution of the BVP, where the infinite sum on the right-hand side below represents a particular solution ${y}_{p}$ of the differential equation.\n\n*Hint: In this specific problem, the forcing function $f(x)$ is chosen such that the particular solution ${y}_{p}$ satisfies ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0.$*\n\n$$\ny(x)∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We write the solution as $y={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}$, where ${y}_{c}$ solves the homogeneous equation and ${y}_{p}$ is a particular solution.\n\nFirst, solve the homogeneous equation ${y}^{″}+5y=0.$ Its general solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).\n$$\n\nNow, we use the hint. Since ${y}_{p}(0)=0$ and ${y}_{p}(1)=0$, we have\n\n$$\ny(0)={y}_{c}(0)\\,\\text{and}\\,y(1)={y}_{c}(1).\n$$\n\nNext, we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Use $y(0)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)+{y}_{p}(0) \\\\ 0 & =A(1)+B(0)+0 \\\\ A & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{c}(x)=B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x).$\n\n- Use $y(1)=6$ with $A=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(1) & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+{y}_{p}(1) \\\\ 6 & =B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})+0 \\\\ B & =\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)∼\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)+{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-91b8581d-d97f-5540-9faf-641daf95fba0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-91b8581d-d97f-5540-9faf-641daf95fba0" :answer-field/id #uuid "91b8581d-d97f-5540-9faf-641daf95fba0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/select :answer-field/correct "answer-c1e68085-55c3-57a9-aaa0-14e18af0e330" :answer-field/choices ["answer-b5605cf5-4e40-5482-98b8-1d348f6bdb6a" "answer-ad7031cc-3266-50a5-b23e-e80324be569a" "answer-8db0a3b8-eaf2-565b-a867-fb89aa025843" "answer-c1e68085-55c3-57a9-aaa0-14e18af0e330"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b5605cf5-4e40-5482-98b8-1d348f6bdb6a" :answer/id #uuid "b5605cf5-4e40-5482-98b8-1d348f6bdb6a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2)}cos\\,(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ad7031cc-3266-50a5-b23e-e80324be569a" :answer/id #uuid "ad7031cc-3266-50a5-b23e-e80324be569a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8db0a3b8-eaf2-565b-a867-fb89aa025843" :answer/id #uuid "8db0a3b8-eaf2-565b-a867-fb89aa025843" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2)}sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1e68085-55c3-57a9-aaa0-14e18af0e330" :answer/id #uuid "c1e68085-55c3-57a9-aaa0-14e18af0e330" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{6}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5})}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\sqrt{5}x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-96850cf5-d4b6-549d-855c-af0332a0f248" :activity/id #uuid "96850cf5-d4b6-549d-855c-af0332a0f248" :activity/title "Solving BVPs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186050814 :activity/course 17592186046644 :activity/steps ["step-571e314f-a98c-570d-9438-f25fd9bdb86f" "step-3cad45fa-b76b-5d49-807e-179e9cf7aed9" "step-c8eee27f-b98e-539a-b55c-614e4d95051f" "step-de0f6281-e792-51d2-a694-b1b34794e517" "step-817024dc-13d6-502b-82e0-d9f5442ed8f4"] :activity/first-step "step-571e314f-a98c-570d-9438-f25fd9bdb86f" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-571e314f-a98c-570d-9438-f25fd9bdb86f" :step/id #uuid "571e314f-a98c-570d-9438-f25fd9bdb86f" :step/content 17592186231573 :step/next "step-3cad45fa-b76b-5d49-807e-179e9cf7aed9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-3cad45fa-b76b-5d49-807e-179e9cf7aed9" :step/id #uuid "3cad45fa-b76b-5d49-807e-179e9cf7aed9" :step/content 17592186231575 :step/next "step-c8eee27f-b98e-539a-b55c-614e4d95051f" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-c8eee27f-b98e-539a-b55c-614e4d95051f" :step/id #uuid "c8eee27f-b98e-539a-b55c-614e4d95051f" :step/content 17592186372854 :step/next "step-de0f6281-e792-51d2-a694-b1b34794e517" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-de0f6281-e792-51d2-a694-b1b34794e517" :step/id #uuid "de0f6281-e792-51d2-a694-b1b34794e517" :step/content "question-a25da531-efbb-5c52-a54e-e70d588e0c03" :step/next "step-817024dc-13d6-502b-82e0-d9f5442ed8f4" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-817024dc-13d6-502b-82e0-d9f5442ed8f4" :step/id #uuid "817024dc-13d6-502b-82e0-d9f5442ed8f4" :step/content 17592186372878 :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105870 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-c171c6db-2b08-53c8-ae94-2e1ffe80b16e" "question-a56eecfc-87ce-57b1-87a4-1fa356b28d07"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186105872 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-efe752cf-6c60-5a23-aae8-55bc453cac9b" "question-e5a09c22-28d0-55d4-9b16-abdb75945ec5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-c171c6db-2b08-53c8-ae94-2e1ffe80b16e" :question/id #uuid "c171c6db-2b08-53c8-ae94-2e1ffe80b16e" :question/math-academy-id "q-171954" :question/problem "![](images/f2/f2c8ddcb1cddce59fbecf25ea606e8400d04d896dfdb263dd0aaf4e6c7de682f.png)\n\nThe area of the triangle $△ABC$ is{{field-1}}\n${cm}^{2}.$\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" :question/worked-solution "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is \n\n$$\nb=AB=12\\,\\text{cm}\n$$\n\nand the corresponding height is \n\n$$\nh=6\\,\\text{cm}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 6}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{72}{2} \\\\  & =36\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-04ecb164-1b59-540d-9f7b-f7200e5ea844"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-04ecb164-1b59-540d-9f7b-f7200e5ea844" :answer-field/id #uuid "04ecb164-1b59-540d-9f7b-f7200e5ea844" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e6f74edc-39f7-5577-9d11-30854b64a223" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e6f74edc-39f7-5577-9d11-30854b64a223"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6f74edc-39f7-5577-9d11-30854b64a223" :answer/id #uuid "e6f74edc-39f7-5577-9d11-30854b64a223" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a56eecfc-87ce-57b1-87a4-1fa356b28d07" :question/id #uuid "a56eecfc-87ce-57b1-87a4-1fa356b28d07" :question/math-academy-id "q-53149" :question/problem "![](images/f6/f6b58865cc7048560bc547ed03b95977d5f9ed013cb411991a82d7da140717f7.png)\n\nFind the area of the triangle $△ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" :question/worked-solution "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is \n\n$$\nb=AC=5\n$$\n\nand the corresponding height is\n\n$$\nh=BC=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\cdot 8}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{2} \\\\  & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c8b73006-e09d-58b6-a70a-2960668f8428"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c8b73006-e09d-58b6-a70a-2960668f8428" :answer-field/id #uuid "c8b73006-e09d-58b6-a70a-2960668f8428" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4d17b541-3b1e-5cad-8b55-90c653d704ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-98f0d08c-fbce-51d0-be7d-b549e908a1ae" "answer-def9d678-fcf0-5088-bf30-db47e355c75b" "answer-4d17b541-3b1e-5cad-8b55-90c653d704ef" "answer-c49b086b-700a-59dd-9be4-a6fcd55efb2b" "answer-a5b4c832-5b2a-5c57-93d5-64555490cd2c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98f0d08c-fbce-51d0-be7d-b549e908a1ae" :answer/id #uuid "98f0d08c-fbce-51d0-be7d-b549e908a1ae" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-def9d678-fcf0-5088-bf30-db47e355c75b" :answer/id #uuid "def9d678-fcf0-5088-bf30-db47e355c75b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d17b541-3b1e-5cad-8b55-90c653d704ef" :answer/id #uuid "4d17b541-3b1e-5cad-8b55-90c653d704ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c49b086b-700a-59dd-9be4-a6fcd55efb2b" :answer/id #uuid "c49b086b-700a-59dd-9be4-a6fcd55efb2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "30" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5b4c832-5b2a-5c57-93d5-64555490cd2c" :answer/id #uuid "a5b4c832-5b2a-5c57-93d5-64555490cd2c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "8" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-efe752cf-6c60-5a23-aae8-55bc453cac9b" :question/id #uuid "efe752cf-6c60-5a23-aae8-55bc453cac9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-117368" :question/problem "![](images/b6/b66dea0994329cf63ff08137293efc33fe994a5ae0395d661dc014044a1c0d14.png)\n\nConsider the triangles $△ABC$ and $△CDE$ shown above. If the area of $△CDE$ is $27{\\text{ cm}}^{2}$, find the area of $△ABC.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" :question/worked-solution "Let's consider $△CDE.$ We can see that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is the height corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{DE}$ and that $DE=6\\text{ cm}.$ \n\nSince the area of $△CDE$ is $27{\\text{ cm}}^{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 27 & =\\frac{6\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\ 6CD & =54 \\\\ CD & =9\\text{ cm}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that $\\overset{―}{CD}$ is also the height of $△ABC$ corresponding to the base $\\overset{―}{AB}.$\n\n![](images/95/95e84873b1353e6a04bb4e6e7df5f45c09eb4751b1170d6eaf8a9bcfa4b572de.png)\n\nTherefore, we find the area of $△ABC$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{AB\\cdot CD}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot 9}{2} \\\\  & =18{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b79f9a3f-6b08-5799-b574-1481f53da89e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b79f9a3f-6b08-5799-b574-1481f53da89e" :answer-field/id #uuid "b79f9a3f-6b08-5799-b574-1481f53da89e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-59d1971b-9d87-547d-b44e-e2249b87b679" :answer-field/choices ["answer-59d1971b-9d87-547d-b44e-e2249b87b679" "answer-aebe24a0-a982-5e43-ac8e-d6f9ef609c23" "answer-d3a4eb50-2a30-58e7-a4c5-d6f5478d5a3a" "answer-4522ce6c-ceb2-59af-8de1-031e99eb3fcd" "answer-41f96e27-bdd7-5af5-85cd-a7d4cae8cf15"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-59d1971b-9d87-547d-b44e-e2249b87b679" :answer/id #uuid "59d1971b-9d87-547d-b44e-e2249b87b679" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "18{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-aebe24a0-a982-5e43-ac8e-d6f9ef609c23" :answer/id #uuid "aebe24a0-a982-5e43-ac8e-d6f9ef609c23" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "22{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3a4eb50-2a30-58e7-a4c5-d6f5478d5a3a" :answer/id #uuid "d3a4eb50-2a30-58e7-a4c5-d6f5478d5a3a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "26{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4522ce6c-ceb2-59af-8de1-031e99eb3fcd" :answer/id #uuid "4522ce6c-ceb2-59af-8de1-031e99eb3fcd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "36{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-41f96e27-bdd7-5af5-85cd-a7d4cae8cf15" :answer/id #uuid "41f96e27-bdd7-5af5-85cd-a7d4cae8cf15" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9{\\text{ cm}}^{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-e5a09c22-28d0-55d4-9b16-abdb75945ec5" :question/id #uuid "e5a09c22-28d0-55d4-9b16-abdb75945ec5" :question/math-academy-id "q-21055" :question/problem "![](images/b1/b12eaaaf9aafa591bef00ca1b11d24783fae4a46f7253bddb05b9b206dbcb9e6.png)\n\nFind $HB$ given that the area of $△ABC$ is $140\\,{m}^{2},$  $AH=15\\,m,$ and $CH=14\\,m.$\n\n(Figure not drawn to scale)" :question/worked-solution "The area of the triangle is defined as \n\n$$\nA=\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2},\n$$\n\n where $b$ is a base and $h$ is the corresponding height.\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the base is $b=AB,$ and the corresponding height is \n\n$$\nh=CH=14\\,m.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the formula for the area, we can solve for the base as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{b\\cdot h}{2} \\\\ 140 & =\\frac{b\\cdot 14}{2} \\\\ 280 & =b\\cdot 14 \\\\ b & =\\frac{280}{14} \\\\ b & =20\\,m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $AB=20\\,m$ and hence, we have\n\n$$\nHB=AB-AH=20-15=5\\,m.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-e6c4c9c5-20e2-5ad2-b45c-aab945f75318"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e6c4c9c5-20e2-5ad2-b45c-aab945f75318" :answer-field/id #uuid "e6c4c9c5-20e2-5ad2-b45c-aab945f75318" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-54b437df-237e-5cbe-901f-e3dec8ac46ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-00382e30-a564-55e8-904d-5fcee95e154d" "answer-4e8497f6-7f80-528c-9ccc-fe05a60dca49" "answer-fc8aa577-1c0d-575c-ac59-74c578543ac8" "answer-2d63a2ed-5ba5-5201-bbcd-120a70c032c6" "answer-54b437df-237e-5cbe-901f-e3dec8ac46ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00382e30-a564-55e8-904d-5fcee95e154d" :answer/id #uuid "00382e30-a564-55e8-904d-5fcee95e154d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.5\\,m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4e8497f6-7f80-528c-9ccc-fe05a60dca49" :answer/id #uuid "4e8497f6-7f80-528c-9ccc-fe05a60dca49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4.8\\,m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc8aa577-1c0d-575c-ac59-74c578543ac8" :answer/id #uuid "fc8aa577-1c0d-575c-ac59-74c578543ac8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\,m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2d63a2ed-5ba5-5201-bbcd-120a70c032c6" :answer/id #uuid "2d63a2ed-5ba5-5201-bbcd-120a70c032c6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5.2\\,m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-54b437df-237e-5cbe-901f-e3dec8ac46ef" :answer/id #uuid "54b437df-237e-5cbe-901f-e3dec8ac46ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5\\,m" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186106016 :activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124651 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-ec111185-2cdb-5c2e-acc4-77d77fc0a299" "question-fcd8e2d4-6c09-5241-9d34-1a3c5fb626c5"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124652 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-77084529-91b2-5a0f-94a4-b8df58237bc4"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-ec111185-2cdb-5c2e-acc4-77d77fc0a299" :question/id #uuid "ec111185-2cdb-5c2e-acc4-77d77fc0a299" :question/math-academy-id "q-164405" :question/problem "Identify the surface $z=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+{y}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the equation of an elliptic paraboloid centered at $O$ is given by\n\n$$\nz=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is an elliptic paraboloid with $a=\\sqrt{2},\\,b=1.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ab2ae139-0331-5324-aecb-f288099bc828"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ab2ae139-0331-5324-aecb-f288099bc828" :answer-field/id #uuid "ab2ae139-0331-5324-aecb-f288099bc828" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f95335eb-e1cd-5c8c-ad78-e2ddd9e2e25c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-27489e9f-c2eb-558f-939e-5dd34b995e02" "answer-7935dad6-005d-5185-a923-ca88faca7e6d" "answer-0a290a76-571a-572b-970a-a7b94abaf475" "answer-b9aad18a-8e8b-52e7-a8c7-34b28427a3dd" "answer-f95335eb-e1cd-5c8c-ad78-e2ddd9e2e25c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-27489e9f-c2eb-558f-939e-5dd34b995e02" :answer/id #uuid "27489e9f-c2eb-558f-939e-5dd34b995e02" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A cylinder" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7935dad6-005d-5185-a923-ca88faca7e6d" :answer/id #uuid "7935dad6-005d-5185-a923-ca88faca7e6d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A hyperbolic paraboloid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0a290a76-571a-572b-970a-a7b94abaf475" :answer/id #uuid "0a290a76-571a-572b-970a-a7b94abaf475" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A sphere" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b9aad18a-8e8b-52e7-a8c7-34b28427a3dd" :answer/id #uuid "b9aad18a-8e8b-52e7-a8c7-34b28427a3dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An elliptic cone" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f95335eb-e1cd-5c8c-ad78-e2ddd9e2e25c" :answer/id #uuid "f95335eb-e1cd-5c8c-ad78-e2ddd9e2e25c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An elliptic paraboloid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fcd8e2d4-6c09-5241-9d34-1a3c5fb626c5" :question/id #uuid "fcd8e2d4-6c09-5241-9d34-1a3c5fb626c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-164478" :question/problem "Identify the following surface: \n\n$$\n25(x-1)^{2}+16(y-2)^{2}=1,\\,-\\infty <z<\\infty \n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the equation of an elliptic cylinder, whose axis of symmetry is the $z$-axis, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1,\n$$\n\nand an elliptic cylinder, whose axis of symmetry is parallel to the $z$-axis and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ in the $xy$-plane, is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-{x}_{0})^{2}}{{a}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-{y}_{0})^{2}}{{b}^{2}}=1.\n$$\n\nThus, our equation is an elliptic cylinder with the following parameters:\n\n$$\n({x}_{0},{y}_{0})=(1,2),\\,a=\\frac{1}{5},\\,b=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-ca554985-239e-5793-9c9c-69a9311b055f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-ca554985-239e-5793-9c9c-69a9311b055f" :answer-field/id #uuid "ca554985-239e-5793-9c9c-69a9311b055f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-548e73f6-f1a1-5e49-a0bb-8083290eeb6e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ce6d9bd6-cb84-5c92-9e76-0e8f8e539455" "answer-689b393f-cf28-5e91-8ee1-b5e413b6e464" "answer-4afa42bc-1bc6-55a1-836f-02d7bfe3748d" "answer-efe9a0ac-dcaa-5d9e-a1d5-9f71fcce64ac" "answer-548e73f6-f1a1-5e49-a0bb-8083290eeb6e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce6d9bd6-cb84-5c92-9e76-0e8f8e539455" :answer/id #uuid "ce6d9bd6-cb84-5c92-9e76-0e8f8e539455" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A hyperbolic cylinder" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-689b393f-cf28-5e91-8ee1-b5e413b6e464" :answer/id #uuid "689b393f-cf28-5e91-8ee1-b5e413b6e464" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A parabolic cylinder" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4afa42bc-1bc6-55a1-836f-02d7bfe3748d" :answer/id #uuid "4afa42bc-1bc6-55a1-836f-02d7bfe3748d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An ellipsoid" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-efe9a0ac-dcaa-5d9e-a1d5-9f71fcce64ac" :answer/id #uuid "efe9a0ac-dcaa-5d9e-a1d5-9f71fcce64ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An elliptic cone" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-548e73f6-f1a1-5e49-a0bb-8083290eeb6e" :answer/id #uuid "548e73f6-f1a1-5e49-a0bb-8083290eeb6e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An elliptic cylinder" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-77084529-91b2-5a0f-94a4-b8df58237bc4" :question/id #uuid "77084529-91b2-5a0f-94a4-b8df58237bc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-164545" :question/problem "Identify the following surface:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=-z,\\,-\\infty <y<\\infty .\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the equation of a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $z$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py.\n$$\n\nThus, a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $y$**-axis** is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4pz.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our equation is a parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $y$-axis and $p=-\\frac{1}{4}.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-f07fcd87-80e3-5f3c-9725-097e537688aa"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-f07fcd87-80e3-5f3c-9725-097e537688aa" :answer-field/id #uuid "f07fcd87-80e3-5f3c-9725-097e537688aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e384968e-2b68-56ca-ac8a-00e5e9f4fac0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-954d555a-b828-5a64-8e5f-985796f531d7" "answer-e2ecc1df-530c-5316-a1e3-995deb117c17" "answer-e384968e-2b68-56ca-ac8a-00e5e9f4fac0" "answer-297953e7-2272-5f4c-8f5d-c7a5ba63a267" "answer-bc0a3e75-2d22-5d28-a469-369d9ac74889"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-954d555a-b828-5a64-8e5f-985796f531d7" :answer/id #uuid "954d555a-b828-5a64-8e5f-985796f531d7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A couple of planes" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e2ecc1df-530c-5316-a1e3-995deb117c17" :answer/id #uuid "e2ecc1df-530c-5316-a1e3-995deb117c17" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $x$-axis" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e384968e-2b68-56ca-ac8a-00e5e9f4fac0" :answer/id #uuid "e384968e-2b68-56ca-ac8a-00e5e9f4fac0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "A parabolic cylinder whose axis is the $y$-axis" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-297953e7-2272-5f4c-8f5d-c7a5ba63a267" :answer/id #uuid "297953e7-2272-5f4c-8f5d-c7a5ba63a267" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An elliptic cylinder whose axis is the $x$-axis" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc0a3e75-2d22-5d28-a469-369d9ac74889" :answer/id #uuid "bc0a3e75-2d22-5d28-a469-369d9ac74889" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "An elliptic cylinder whose axis is the $y$-axis" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125888 :tutorial/content "The most common quadric surfaces are those centered at the origin. We describe these common surfaces in the table below.\n\n| Surface | Equation | Image |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Ellipsoid | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}+\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1$ | ![](images/09/09dc100248a28a5518bcd8428368edefc7c3ee8d5ef0e50515cb7552ee54a376.png) |\n| Elliptic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/76/76e4ad8cb1e83eadae4dce865e100c9a716f9830e7f12adb367b2a9b74d1a651.png) |\n| Hyperbolic Paraboloid | $z = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/cb/cbc04d7f98e1bb7ce0d62eda71102a6abad708dfcedd7aabee8fff23281471dd.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of One Sheet | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1$ | ![](images/df/df6ef914bc78e121d75baf9f5147784d0e6e434d76800eff60dcb3c3d764477c.png) |\n| Hyperboloid of Two Sheets | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=-1$ | ![](images/08/0870b95c5ce1133d6645e3063174157e36c4354df370c05e48218111ea714838.png) |\n| Elliptic Cone | $\\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}$ | ![](images/0f/0f492bf07e573ec7858977e48fa4bf81635e61520b5caa9edef14bd259184347.png) |\n\nAlso, recall the following from previous lessons:\n\n- We can generate paraboloids, hyperboloids, and cones with alternative orientation axes by permuting the variables in the corresponding equation. \n\nFor example, the equation of an elliptic cone with its vertex at $O$ and axis of symmetry along the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = \\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}.\n$$\n\n- We can shift the center/vertex/saddle point of a quadric surface to $(x_0,y_0,z_0)$ using the usual coordinate transformation:\n\n$$\n(x,y,z) \\longrightarrow (x-x_0,y-y_0,z-z_0)\n$$\n\nFinally, there is a simple way to distinguish between hyperboloids with one and two sheets by simply looking at the equations:\n\n- *one* minus sign indicates hyperboloids of *one* sheet:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}=1\n$$\n\n- *two* minus signs indicate hyperboloids of *two* sheets:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-} \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2}= \\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}-}1\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125883 :tutorial/content "A table showing the most common cylinders whose base curve is centered at the origin in the $xy$-plane is given below.\n\n| Surface | Equation | Image |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Elliptic Cylinder | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}+\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2} = 1$ | ![](images/2b/2b8788f027b93593a025542face33546425b057d1eb3b8e18da26e7774f6ed20.png) |\n| Parabolic Cylinder | $x^2=4py$\n\n$y^2=4px$ | ![](images/d3/d3f31aae01222e6a63e5de185c5b9f5a8af5aed8a750c6bc230e20e9fd93871a.png) |\n| Hyperbolic Cylinder | $\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}-\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}=1$\n\n$\\dfrac{y^2}{b^2}-\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2}=1$ | ![](images/15/159bb67600e079d9943ba6f79fcc6e580e3718f822dce50620c2bb6d2d9b76c8.png) |\n\nSimilar to quadric surfaces:\n\n- We can generate cylinders with alternative orientation axes by describing a base curve in the $xz$-or $yz$-planes. \n\nFor example, the equation of an elliptic cylinder with its axis of symmetry along the $y$-axis is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x^2}{a^2} + \\dfrac{z^2}{c^2} = 1.\n$$\n\n- We can shift the center/vertex of the base curve in the usual way." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124700 :activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054664 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-688b04b8-3e75-5482-8d9f-399abc4be90f" "question-fd6b1704-ef29-5746-9fcc-ddfee319c814" "question-43f4b257-b0e8-5f30-8dc0-cac9bfb6fbb7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054561 :question/problem "![](images/f1/f1f5b00d57d5cac975bbd06dffcbae025f535fa3a2b36054d5cb974f7e466aa2.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solution(s) of the following systems of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=-{x}^{2}+2 \\\\ y=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/9f/9fe6da31061120bc2b654e64dedcfde1ca62beb2a96535031d302d11590747c1.png)\n\nFrom the graph, the curves intersect at \n\n$\\qquad$ $(-2,-2)\\quad$ and $\\quad(2,-2).$ \n\nTherefore, these are the solutions of the corresponding system." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054665 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-6f0c806d-87b9-56dc-bfb8-0487c34983b3" "question-74d5d63f-ce92-5d6a-9703-105dc97e875a"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186054562 :question/problem "![](images/f0/f0d34cabb9fb17222b551bcb3d85b246e9ea6abfcbef9ccdddd8096f35db0bab.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=f(x) \\\\ y=g(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/7f/7fb973cca11131914902270aeb80ee32ad18071e2df73243ad0912b494c695af.png)\n\nFrom the graph, the curves intersect at \n\n$\\qquad$ $(-1,-1)\\quad$ and $\\quad(0,-1).$ \n\nTherefore, these are the solutions of the corresponding system." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-688b04b8-3e75-5482-8d9f-399abc4be90f" :question/id #uuid "688b04b8-3e75-5482-8d9f-399abc4be90f" :question/math-academy-id "q-78288" :question/problem "![](images/9f/9fbc46d5a81532e274d9f59832db14730169905d383dccb61d0039b2bc89ab31.png)\n\nUsing the graphical method, find the solutions $(x,y)$ of the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}y=2 \\\\ y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}-1\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The solutions $(x,y)$ of the system correspond to the intersection points of the two curves.\n\n![](images/83/83a2271a5f0620bcb97698d8b04367e6935f1e8cd6c25f6d2538aeb411c75a9f.png)\n\nWe see from the graph that the curves intersect at \n\n$\\,$ $(-3,2)\\,$ and $\\,(3,2).$ \n\nTherefore, these are the solutions of the corresponding system." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-072934f3-5801-547f-ad24-fcd5b1f8aca6"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-072934f3-5801-547f-ad24-fcd5b1f8aca6" :answer-field/id #uuid "072934f3-5801-547f-ad24-fcd5b1f8aca6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d1f3bb21-c0e2-514d-934d-4534235c6e07" :answer-field/choices ["answer-385d13e6-8131-5aa4-ba0d-ef23ab46ca4d" "answer-f155f4e4-cff4-535d-b740-6a4f89852af0" "answer-9ca51bf2-6d17-5485-9398-bd76c8de93df" "answer-b3b99647-8e60-5b5c-ac6e-a328873ff39c" "answer-d1f3bb21-c0e2-514d-934d-4534235c6e07"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-385d13e6-8131-5aa4-ba0d-ef23ab46ca4d" :answer/id #uuid "385d13e6-8131-5aa4-ba0d-ef23ab46ca4d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,2)$ and $(1,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f155f4e4-cff4-535d-b740-6a4f89852af0" :answer/id #uuid "f155f4e4-cff4-535d-b740-6a4f89852af0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-1,3)$ and $(1,3)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9ca51bf2-6d17-5485-9398-bd76c8de93df" :answer/id #uuid "9ca51bf2-6d17-5485-9398-bd76c8de93df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(-2,2)$ and $(2,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b3b99647-